\end{figure}

%The secondary interaction of the NCQE reaction is approached through a comparative analysis of experimental data concerning neutron-oxygen interaction and simulation models.
Our approach to the secondary interaction of the NCQE reaction is by comparing the experimental data of neutron oxygen interaction and the simulation models of it.
Therefore, we performed MC simulations with some different hadron inelastic models compatible with Geant4.

%Generally, in the energy region of interest of the NCQE neutron (20 MeV to 1 GeV), the final state products of a single hadron inelastic interaction are sequentially determined by a combination of intranuclear cascade and deexcitation model.
In the energy region of interest in a water Cherenkov detector, e.g., the NCQE neutron (20 MeV to 1 GeV) in SK, the final state products of a single hadron inelastic interaction are determined sequentially by a combination of intranuclear cascade and deexcitation model.
As a cascade model, Bertini cascade (\verb|BERT|), Binary cascade (\verb|BIC|), and the Liege intranuclear cascade (\verb|INCL++|) are implemented and available in Geant4.
Note that the simulation causing the large uncertainty on the NCQE prediction is based on \verb|GCALOR| in Geant3, which is essentially identical to \verb|BERT| in Geant4. 

%In general, the cascade model determines products of the interaction by an avalanche of binary collisions of a projectile and the nucleons within the target nucleus. 
%Binary particle-particle collisions are subject to Pauli blocking. 
%Emission of hadrons and light clusters is possible; light clusters, in particular, are produced via a dynamical phase-space coalescence algorithm. 
%The cascade stops when all cascade particles have either left or been trapped in the nucleus for the BERT and BIC.

The cascade model is generally used to determine products of interactions through an avalanche of binary collisions between a projectile and the nucleons within a target nucleus.
Pauli blocking is an inherent property of binary particle-particle collisions.
The emission of hadrons and light clusters follows a theoretical possibility. 
In particular, light clusters are produced via a dynamical phase-space coalescence algorithm.
The cascade is halted when all cascade particles have either departed or become trapped in the nucleus, as observed in the BERT and BIC models.
On the other hand, INCL++ terminates the cascade by the time self-consistently defined as $t_{\mathrm{stop}} = t_0 \left( A_{\mathrm{target}}/{208} \right)^{0.16}$,  where $t_0$ is a parameter set as 70 fm/c, and $A_{\mathrm{target}}$ is the mass number of the target nucleus~\cite{incl}. 
%\begin{equation}
%    t_{\mathrm{stop}} = t_0 \left( \frac{A_{\mathrm{target}}}{208} \right)^{0.16},
%\end{equation}

%After the cascade stage, a pre-equilibrium phase of nuclear reaction is considered until the system reaches equilibrium by state transitions with light particle emission up to alpha.
%This treatment is implemented in a part of \verb|BERT| based on the statistical Griffin exciton model.
%For \verb|BIC| and \verb|INCL++|, the semi-classical exciton model based on the Griffin model is implemented in G4PreCompoundModel as a backend for them.
%Therefore, there is almost no difference between the three cascade models in this point.
Subsequent to the cascade stage, a pre-equilibrium phase of nuclear reaction ensues, persisting until the system attains equilibrium by undergoing state transitions accompanied by light particle emission up to alpha.
This treatment is implemented in a part of \verb|BERT| based on the statistical Griffin exciton model~\cite{griffin}.
For the semi-classical exciton model based on the Griffin model, the implementation of both BIC and INCL++ occurs within G4PreCompoundModel as a backend.
Consequently, there is negligible variation among the three cascade models at this particular point.

In the equilibrium stage, the residual nucleus in the excited state relaxes by particle emission (in the case of oxygen, gamma-ray and nucleons), fission, or other mechanisms.
In order to simulate the deexcitation process, the cascade models are coupled with one of the separate statistical deexcitation codes.
The native evaporation model implemented in \verb|BERT| based on the Weisskopf statistical theory~\cite{weisskopf}, which computes the emission of particles until the excitation energy reaches a cutoff~\cite{g4bert}. 
%In photon evaporation, for instance, it successively emits gamma-rays with an energy sampled from an exponential-like function until the excitation energy satisfies a condition $E^{*} < E^{\gamma}_{cutoff} = 10^{-15}$ MeV~\cite{dostrovsky}.
%No discrete gamma-ray energy corresponding to the nuclear levels is considered in the implementation.
%Note that particle emission by Fermi break-up is not applicable for oxygen and carbon due to the limitation on the mass number $A < 12$.
In the process of photon evaporation, for example, successive emissions of gamma-rays are observed, with the energy of each emission derived from an exponential-like function. This process continues until the excitation energy reaches a threshold value, defined by $E^{*} < E^{\gamma}_{cutoff} = 10^{-15}$ MeV~\cite{dostrovsky}.
In the implementation, no discrete gamma-ray energy corresponding to the nuclear levels is considered.
It is important to note that the process of particle emission resulting from Fermi break-up does not apply to oxygen and carbon due to the limitation on the mass number, $A < 12$.

%On the other hand, the default deexcitation model of \verb|BIC| and \verb|INCL++| consists mainly of G4Evaporation (for particle emission) and G4PhotonEvaporation (for gamma-ray emission) utilized in G4PreCompoundModel.
%It mainly computes particle emission up to alpha based on the Weisskopf-Ewing model in the case of oxygen and carbon. 
%There are alternative candidates of the deexcitation model available for \verb|INCL++|.
Conversely, the default deexcitation model of \verb|BIC| and \verb|INCL++| consists primarily of G4Evaporation (for particle emission) and G4PhotonEvaporation (for gamma-ray emission), which are utilized in G4PreCompoundModel.
The primary function of the model is to compute particle emission up to alpha particle based on the Weisskopf-Ewing model in the case of oxygen and carbon.
It should be noted that there are alternative candidates of the deexcitation model available for \verb|INCL++|.
%\verb|ABLA++|, a translation to C++ of the Fortran ABLA07, simulates the deexcitation by calculating the probabilities for emitting gamma-rays, neutrons, light-charged particles, and intermediate-mass fragments according to Weisskopf’s formalism~\cite{abla}.
ABLA++, a translation to C++ of the Fortran-based ABLA07, simulates the deexcitation by calculating the probabilities for emitting gamma-rays, neutrons, light-charged particles, and intermediate-mass fragments according to Weisskopf's formalism~\cite{abla}.
Another candidate is NucDeEx~\cite{nucdeex}, recently developed as a nuclear deexcitation event generator for neutrino interactions and nucleon decays.
%It handles particle emission in case of the excitation energy above the separation energy and multi-hole states based on the branching ratios calculated by the TALYS code~\cite{talys}.
%TALYS computes the branching ratios with the Hauser-Feshbach statistical model~\cite{hfmodel}, which considers angular momentum conservation in contrast with the other (Weisskopf-based) models.
It has been demonstrated to manage particle emission in scenarios where excitation energy exceeds separation energy and multi-hole states, contingent on the branching ratios calculated by the TALYS code~\cite{talys}.
TALYS employs the Hauser-Feshbach statistical model~\cite{hfmodel} to compute branching ratios considering angular momentum conservation, in contrast to other models based on the Weisskopf theory.
Gamma-ray emission is called if the excitation energy is between the first excited state and the separation energy.
The excited state and its branching ratio employed in NucDeEx are based on the experimental data, e.g., ${}^{15}\mathrm{N}^{*}$ and ${}^{11}\mathrm{B}^{*}$.
%Instead, for ${}^{15}\mathrm{O}^{*}$ and ${}^{11}\mathrm{C}^{*}$, the same branching ratio as ${}^{15}\mathrm{N}^{*}$ and ${}^{11}\mathrm{B}^{*}$ are assumed in analoguos with isospin symmetry except for the Coulomb potential difference of proton and neutron due to no experimental data.
In contrast, the branching ratio for ${}^{15}\mathrm{O}^{*}$ and ${}^{11}\mathrm{C}^{*}$ is assumed to be the same as that for ${}^{15}\mathrm{N}^{*}$ and ${}^{11}\mathrm{B}^{*}$.
This assumption is made in the context of isospin symmetry, except for the Coulomb potential difference between protons and neutrons, due to the absence of experimental data in this area.

A summary of the combinations of intranuclear cascade and deexcitation models which we investigated is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:models}.
Note that neutron reactions below 20~MeV are managed by the \verb|G4ParticleHP|, enabling the high precision neutron transportation based on the evaluated nuclear database.
\verb|G4NDL4.5| which primarily refers to ENDF/B-VII.1 was utilized in this study.


