\section{Result}
%In this section, the simulation results with different pairs of intranuclear cascade and deexcitation model are compared with the experimental dataset of the E487 and E525 experiment.
%The data reproducibility of the MC simulation is evaluated by a chi-squared test in the observed gamma-ray spectra in the gamma-ray detector.
%The neutron flux uncertainty of each experiment is considered as a pull term in the chi-squared evaluation:
In this section, a comparative analysis is conducted between the simulation outputs obtained from different intranuclear cascade and deexcitation model pairs and the experimental dataset derived from the E487 and E525 experiments.
The reproducibility of the MC simulation results is assessed through the implementation of a chi-squared test on the observed gamma-ray spectra within the gamma-ray detector.
The neutron flux uncertainty of each experiment is considered a pull term in the chi-squared evaluation as:
\begin{equation}
    \chi^2 = \sum^{bins}_{i} \left(\frac{X^{\mathrm{Data}}_{i}-\eta \times X^{\mathrm{MC}}_{i}}{\sigma^{\mathrm{stat.}}_{i}}\right)^2 + \left( \frac{1-\eta}{\sigma^{\mathrm{flux}}} \right)^2,
    \label{eq:chi2}
\end{equation}
where $X_{i}$ is the count at $i$-th bin of the spectra, $\sigma^{\mathrm{stat.}}$ is the statistical uncertainty of the data, $\eta$ is the nuisance parameter representing the pull term constrainned by the neutron flux uncertainty $\sigma^{\mathrm{flux}}$ of each experiment.

\subsection{Cascade models}
\label{cascade}
%Initially, the Geant4 default pairs of the cascade and deexcitation model are compared to the experimental data.
%Figure~\ref{fig:cascade} shows the gamma-ray energy sepctra of the data (black) and the MC simulations using the models (red: \verb|INCL++|, green: \verb|BIC|, blue: \verb|BERT|) in the case of 30~MeV beam energy (left) and the case of 250~MeV beam energy (right), respectively.
Initially, a comparison is made between the Geant4 default pairs of the cascade and deexcitation model and the experimental data.
As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:cascade}, the gamma-ray energy spectra of the data (black) and the MC simulations using the models (red: INCL++, green: BIC, blue: BERT) are displayed for two different beam energies: 30 MeV (left) and 250 MeV (right).

Table~\ref{tab:cascade} shows a summary of the chi-squared evaluation in each cascade model.
%Because of the definition of the chi-squared in Eq.~\ref{eq:chi2}, the chi-squared value represents a reproducibility in the spectrum shape, and the size of the pull indicates the accuracy of the absolute gamma-ray production by the neutron-nucleus interaction.
%In case of the beam energy at 30~MeV, one can find that \verb|INCL++| and \verb|BIC| showed the similar performance in the spectral shape despite the difference in the size of pull term.
According to the definition of the chi-squared in Eq.~\ref{eq:chi2}, the chi-squared value is indicative of reproducibility in the spectrum shape, and the magnitude of the pull signifies the precision of the absolute gamma-ray production by the neutron-nucleus interaction.
For beam energies of 30~MeV, the performance of INCL++ and BIC in terms of spectral shape is comparable, despite the disparity in the magnitude of the pull term.
%\verb|BERT| largely overestimated the gamma-ray production with a continuum component in an exponential function over the entire energy, resulting in the larger $\chi^2$ and pull.
%These tendencies were compromised in case of the beam energy at 250~MeV. 
BERT demonstrated a tendency to overestimate the gamma-ray production with a continuum component in an exponential function over the entire energy range.
This overestimation led to an increase in both the chi-squared statistic and the pull.
In the instance of the 250-MeV beam energy, these tendencies were compromised.
Both \verb|INCL++| and \verb|BIC| predicted almost identical spectral shape and production of gamma-rays, and demonstrated good agreement with the data as shown in the right plot of Fig.~\ref{fig:cascade}.
Although there were inconsistencies in the spectral shape due to the continuum contribution in \verb|BERT|, the dependence of the production rate on the beam energy was converged among the models.
The behavior of \verb|BERT| is natural by considering the implementation of gamma-ray deexcitation as described in~\ref{sec:inelamodel}.
Thus, it leads to an unrealistic prediction of gamma-ray multiplicity in the NCQE simulation, resulting in the discrepancy observed in the measurements~\cite{ncqe_t2k, ncqe_sk6}. 

%In short, \verb|INCL++| gives better prediction than the others in the models provided by Geant4 in the way of gamma-ray production as a result of neutron-oxygen interaction.
%As mentioned above, there are some custom choices of the deexcitation model compatible with \verb|INCL++| in Geant4.
%It is discussed in the following section in detail.
In summary, the INCL++ model demonstrates superior performance in predicting gamma-ray production from neutron-oxygen interactions.
As previously indicated, there are certain customizable options for the deexcitation model that are compatible with INCL++ in Geant4.
This topic will be examined in the subsequent section.

