Primarily, we compared energy spectra of true-generated gamma-rays in neutron-oxygen interaction with different deexcitation models as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:deex_gen}.
%Although the spectra predicted by G4PreCompoundModel (red) and \verb|NucDeEx| (blue) shows a similar shape except for some small differences, \verb|ABLA++| gives a large difference in the entire range of the spectrum.
A comparison of the spectra predicted by G4PreCompoundModel (red) and NucDeEx (blue) reveals a high degree of similarity, except for minor discrepancies.
However, a significant disparity is apparent when ABLA++ is considered, exhibiting substantial variations across the entire spectrum.
%\verb|ABLA++| overpredicts contribution in the gamma-ray energy more than 8~MeV which disagrees with the consistency between G4PreCompoundModel and the experimental data shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:cascade}. 
The present study has demonstrated that the ABLA++ model overpredicts the contribution of the gamma-ray energy by more than 8~MeV. This finding is inconsistent with the observed consistency between G4PreCompoundModel and the experimental data shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:cascade}.
%Therefore, we concluded that \verb|ABLA++| had no capability of prediction of the deexcitation as a result of neutron-oxygen interaction in the purpose of simulation for NCQE in water Cherenkov detector.
Therefore, it was concluded that ABLA++ lacks the capability to predict deexcitation resulting from the neutron-oxygen interaction in the context of simulating NCQE in a water Cherenkov detector.
In the following discussion, ABLA++ is not considered in our investigation.

Finally, the experimental data at the beam energies of 30, 80, and 250~MeV were compared with the MC simulations with different deexcitation models.
Figure~\ref{fig:deex} shows the gamma-ray energy sepctra of the data (black) and the MC simulations using G4PreCompoundModel (red) and NucDeEx (green) coupled with \verb|INCL++| at 30~MeV (left), 80~MeV (center) and 250~MeV beam energy (right), respectively.
Because an overestimation of the neutron flux due to a non-linearity of the proton beam current measurement by the Faraday cup was suspected in the E487 experiment, we independently estimated a correction factor of the neutron flux with respect to the E525 results based on the event rate of 2.2~MeV gamma-ray from neutron capture on hydrogen.
Here, we decided to apply a factor of 5 to the spectrum to correct the flux overestimation.
With the correction, the production cross sections of certain gamma-rays at 80~MeV of the beam energy reported in~\cite{E487} become more consistent with the other measurements~\cite{E525, lang, nelson} than ever.

