Numerical simulations are performed by using the time-splitting spectral method\cite{Bao2006}. According to the current experimental conditions \cite{Mukherjee2022,Fletcher2021}, specifically we consider a BEC composed of $\ ^{23}\mathrm{Na}$ atoms with a trap frequency of $\omega=2\pi\times88.6\ \mathrm{Hz}$, and focus on the case of rapid rotation limit with $\Omega=1$. Therefore, the length unit and time unit correspond to $2.22\ \mathrm{\mu m}$ and $1.80\ \mathrm{ms}$ respectively, while the interaction strength parameter is set as $g=0.0125$. Notice, the interaction strength $gN$ can be regarded as a key control parameter and the analysis in the following can be directly generalized to other parameter regimes.



In this section, we first explore the influence of the initial phase and density distributions on the dynamics of a stable droplet array and give a physical interpretation. Then, we analyze the dynamics of moving droplet arrays that arise from various initial velocity profiles. Finally, we demonstrate the possibility to construct stable 1D droplet arrays at arbitrary positions via designing the initial phase distribution.

In the rotating frame, the BEC experiences a synthetic magnetic field and the vector potential can be expressed as $\boldsymbol{A}=\Omega(0,2x)$ in the Landau gauge\cite{Mukherjee2022,Fletcher2021,Crpel2024}. The LLL exhibits a uniform phase\cite{Barczyk2024,Barsukova2024,Jung2024}, which can be represented as $\varphi=-xy$ in the symmetric gauge $\boldsymbol{A}=\Omega(-y,x)$. Considering the rotational symmetry of Eq.~\ref{equ:GPE2}, for convenience, one can express $\varphi$ in polar coordinates as 
\begin{equation}
\varphi = -\frac{1}{2} r^2 {\rm sin} \left(2\theta\right). 
\label{Lphase}
\end{equation}
Here, $\left(r,\theta\right)$ represents the position in polar coordinates.





First, we investigate the dynamics of various initial wavefunctions as shown in Fig.~ \ref{fig:FIG1}. While the initial phase distribution is set as $\varphi = -r^2 {\rm sin} \left(2\theta\right) /2$ for the strip similar to the experiment~\cite{Mukherjee2022}, the initial density distribution can be various. We find that, all configurations eventually evolve into a stable droplet array, although different initial density distributions at $t=0$ influence the interaction strength at different parts of the condensate. The overall density distribution along the long axis tends to maintain a shape similar to that at the initial time, and even within a single array, the droplets may vary in size. Furthermore, we generalize the above analysis to the density distribution in the shape of the ring, a circular droplet array can be formed with the initial phase distribution $\varphi=r_0^2\theta$. Here, $r_0$ is the radius of the ring in the middle.

Through further calculations, we find that a stable 1D droplet array can be formed in a large parameter region for both the strip and the ring. Taking the strip with a uniform density distribution as an example, the width range is approximately $1\le l_b\le10$ while the interaction strength is approximately $500\le gN\le12000$. Moreover, when the interaction increases, the number of droplets will decrease with the increasing droplet size~\cite{Mukherjee2022}. However, when the initial phase deviates from the form in Eq.~\ref{Lphase}, such as from a quadratic function to a linear one or changed a sine function to a cosine function, the strip will evolve into a chaotic state instead of a droplet array. These results indicate the critical role of the initial phase in the formation of droplet arrays.

In order to interpret the stability of droplet arrays, we investigate the velocity and current distribution in detail during the dynamics. After incorporating the rotational frame correction, the velocity can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
  \boldsymbol{v} =\nabla \varphi-\boldsymbol{A}
\end{equation}

For the phase distribution in Eq.~\ref{Lphase}, the corresponding velocity distribution is $\boldsymbol{v_0}=\left(0,-2x\right)$, which exhibits a sheared profile, analogous to the Kelvin-Helmholtz instability in fluid dynamics, as observed in sheared superfluids\cite{Takeuchi2010,Suzuki2010,Cerfon2016,Baggaley2018,Chomaz2019,Kokubo2021}. Accordingly, the current can be written as $\boldsymbol{j}=\boldsymbol{v}\left|\psi\right|^2$. It can characterize the mass transport of condensates and further reveal the role of sheared flow in the stability and dynamics of droplets. The density and current distribution of a droplet in dynamics are calculated, as displayed in Fig.~\ref{fig:FIG2}, which reveal the droplet is not static but dynamic with a combined motion of rotation and stretching in an approximate period of $4T$. 







In FIG \ref{fig:FIG2}, we present the time evolution of these physical quantities after the formation of droplets. They both oscillate sinusoidally with a period of $T/2$, agreeing well with Eq.~\ref{equ:I2} and ~\ref{equ:LR2}. Actually the dynamics of droplet arrays can be further interpreted in the following perspective. One can construct an individual droplet around a position $(x_0, y_0)$, with the initial wavefunction as
\begin{equation}
   \psi(x,y)=e^{i(-y_0 x+x_0 y)}\psi_0(x-x_0,y-y_0)
   \label{GS}
\end{equation}
Here, $\psi_0(x,y)$ is the ground state with no rotation, i.e.,$\omega=0$. The initial density distribution is approximately Gaussian in weak interaction and parabolic in strong interaction (i.e., the Thomas-Fermi limit). From the dynamics of the density and current distribution (see the SM), one can directly obtain that this droplet has no stretching motion and it just rotates around the point $(x_0,y_0)$, with a period of $T$. Therefore, it can be regarded as a  "static" droplet or the "ground state" of the droplet. Other kinds of droplets contain one or many collective modes such as the breathing mode and the quadrupole mode (see Fig.~\ref{fig:FIG3}). 




This can be seen more clearly in the hydrodynamic equations, which can be expressed as in the rotating frame:
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\partial n}{\partial t} + \nabla\cdot(n {\bf v})=0 
  \label{Hdn}
\end{equation} 
\begin{equation}
  m\frac{\partial {\bf v}}{\partial t} + \nabla [-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m\sqrt{n}}\nabla^2\sqrt{n}+\frac{1}{2}mv^2 +gn ]=0 
  \label{Hdv}
\end{equation} 
Here, $\psi=\sqrt{n}e^{i\phi}$. If one writes the density and the velocity in the form $n=n_s+\delta n$ and ${\bf v}={\bf v}_s+\delta{\bf v}$ respectively, with $n_s$ and ${\bf v}_s$ being the density and the current of the static state in Eq.~\ref{GS}, one can obtain the following equations for the small quantities $\delta n$ and $\delta {\bf v}$
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\partial \delta n}{\partial t} + \nabla\cdot(n \delta{\bf v} -{\bf A}\delta n)=0 
  \label{Hddn}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\partial \delta {\bf v}}{\partial t} + \nabla (\frac{g}{m}\delta n - {\bf A}\cdot\delta{\bf v} ) =0 
  \label{Hddv}
\end{equation}
Here, the quantum pressure term is neglected. By solving Eq.~\ref{Hddn} and \ref{Hddv}, one can obtain various "excited" collective modes around the static state.

In a semiclassical way, analogous to the motion of electrons in a magnetic field, the velocity increases from the droplet center toward its edge. This indicates that the kinetic energy increases as a function of the droplet size. Therefore, when the droplet expands, it will encounter this kinetic energy barrier, which can prevent it from significant expanding and maintain its stability. It implies that such a phenomenon could be a fundamental characteristic of 2D systems under an effective magnetic field.


We have demonstrated that the initial phase distribution plays a crucial role in the formation of droplet arrays and the phase in Eq.~\ref{Lphase} can lead to a stable droplet array. In the following, we investigate the dynamics of moving droplet arrays by changing the initial phase. Specifically, we consider modifying Eq.~\ref{Lphase} as
\begin{equation}
  \varphi =-\frac{1}{2}{r^2}\sin 2\left(\theta  - \beta\right)
\label{equ:phase_r}
\end{equation}
Here, $\beta$ is an adjusting parameter and can be regarded as the angle of rotation referring to the long-axis of the strip.





The dynamics of the strip with various $\beta$ are displayed in Fig.~\ref{fig:FIG4}. For $0<\beta<\pi/4$, the strip undergoes periodic oscillations around the center of the potential trap and evolves into a moving droplet array after some time. When $\beta=\pi/4$, the condensate exhibits a chaotic behavior with no stable dynamical features. For $\pi/4<\beta\le\pi/2$, the strip rotates clockwise around the center of the potential trap and evolves into a moving droplet array. Furthermore, for any $0<\beta<\pi/2$, the condensate exhibits significant periodic contraction and expansion along the long axis. As $\beta$ moves towards $\pi/4$ from $0$ or $\pi/2$, this motion along the long axis becomes more pronounced, which leads to less droplets formed due to the stronger interaction in the moving droplet array. Our results imply that the droplet array can survive even if it moves significantly along the long axis or the short axis.

In order to quantify the motion of the droplet array, we calculate the time evolution of the angle of the long-axis direction for $0<\beta\le\pi/4$ and $\pi/4<\beta\le\pi/2$, as displayed in Fig.~\ref{fig:FIG5}. It can be seen that only when $\beta=0$ or $\beta=\pi/2$ does the result appear linear, corresponding to the stability of the long axis and uniform clockwise rotation, respectively. As $\beta$ deviates more from these two special values, the more intense contraction and expansion along the long axis correspond to larger-scale periodic oscillations or more pronounced nonlinear clockwise rotation. Additionally, for $-\pi/2\le\beta<0$, the order of contraction and expansion along the long axis is reversed. Therefore, the order of speed during the oscillation or rotation process will be reversed, and periodic oscillations will occur within a counterclockwise range.




Moreover, we study the strip with its long axis deviating from the center of the trap. The center of mass follows a uniform circular motion, which can be derived directly from the GPE\cite{GarcaRipoll2001,BialynickiBirula2002,Zhang2007,Bao2013}. In this case, the condensate can also form a stable moving droplet array. Actually, one can obtain a static droplet array in any location by choosing a proper initial phase. For example, inspired by the initial sheared velocity profile with the phase in Eq.~\ref{Lphase}, we design a new phase distribution $  \varphi=-(x-2\Delta x)y$ for the strip centered at position $\Delta x$, which finally can evolve into a static droplet array as expected (see the SM). This further demonstrates the important role of the initial relative sheared velocity in the dynamics of droplet arrays.

Based on this, we propose a new way to construct a stable 1D droplet array via dividing the entire initial condensate into different subsystems, inside which the initial sheared velocity profile is maintained. However, the directions of the velocities in different subsystems are not required to be the same. Therefore, one simple choice of the corresponding phase distributions can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
  \varphi_i =\frac{1}{2}{r^2}\sin 2\left(\theta -\alpha_i\right),i=1,2,...,k
\label{equ:phase_fg}
\end{equation}
Here, $\alpha_i$ represents the polar angle of the center of each subsystem. With this proposal, one static droplet array can be formed at any location in a fairly large parameter region (see the SM).


In Sec \ref{3.2}, by designing the initial phase distribution, a stable 1D droplet array can be constructed at arbitrary positions in the trap. Building on this, we construct possible 2D droplet arrays in the following. First, we focus on a specific 2D scenario where the initial conditions consist of two parallel condensate strips, and illustrate the different dynamical phases in various parameter regimes. Then, we consider to construct more general 2D droplet arrays with new proposals.


We start from the initial state of two parallel condensate strips and calculate the detailed dynamics of droplet arrays. Based on 1D droplet arrays, we design these two condensate strips with the following phase distribution
\begin{equation}
  \varphi=-\left [x-(l_b+l_c){\rm{sgn}}(x)\right]y
\label{equ:phase_dd}
\end{equation}
Here, ${\rm{sgn}}(x)$ is the sign function. In this case, we mainly focus on the effects of the width $l_b$ of a single condensate strip and the distance $l_c$ between them on the dynamics.




We calculate the dynamics of two parallel strips with various widths, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:FIG5}. One can see that the condensate will evolve into a single droplet array (SDA) when the width is small. By increasing the initial width $l_b$, the single droplet array gradually crossovers into a double droplet array (DDA). And finally it becomes chaotic when $l_b$ becomes very large, similar to the 1D droplet array. Moreover, the time evolving into droplets is much larger than the 1D droplet array, which may be due to the more complex particle flow between the two strips. 

The crossover from SDA to DDA can be interpreted in a simple way. Increasing the width $l_b$ will reduce the effective interaction between two strips. Based on the results of the 1D droplet array, within an appropriate initial width range, they can both evolve into a droplet array, resulting in the dynamics of two stable droplet arrays. When the initial width $l_b$ is very large, each strip can not self-organize into droplets but instead exhibit a chaotic behavior. On the other hand, as the initial width $l_b$ is small, the mutual influence between the two strips is very significant, leading to the dynamics of a single droplet array or a intermediate state between SDA and DDA. Actually, one can also obtain the SDA to DDA crossover by gradually increasing the distance between the two strips with a proper width (see the SM). This further support the above interpretation. Because increasing the distance also reduces the interaction between the two strips. Moreover, the time required to form droplets also decreases gradually to that of the 1D case.

Our further study shows that DDA can occur in a large parameter region. In particular, the interaction between droplets become more significant and the droplet arrangement between the two columns can be various, although they are expected to be parallel given the initial translation symmetry.





In Sec \ref{4.1}, we discussed the specific case of two parallel droplet arrays. These conclusions are also applicable to multi-column droplet arrays.  In Fig.~\ref{fig:FIG6}, we display the formation of multi-column droplet arrays in an appropriate parameter region. We can also construct similar results based on the phase condition in Eq.~\ref{equ:phase_dd} (see the SM).

Additionally, through designing a proper distance between the initial condensate density distributions to reduce interactions between droplets, we can construct various 2D droplet lattices, such as the square lattice and the triangular one as displayed in Fig.~\ref{fig:FIG7}. One can use the initial phase distribution in Eq.~\ref{GS} or Eq.~\ref{equ:phase_dd}. This lattice structure is similar to the 2D BEC array prepared in multiple optical potential wells\cite{Huang2024}. However, our droplet arrays are stable in fairly long time. 

Furthermore, we have explored methods for constructing 2D droplet arrays, aiming to provide an appropriate overall phase distribution that allows specific droplet arrays to evolve under a continuous density distribution.  Fig.~\ref{fig:FIG7} also shows the results of the 2D sector-shaped droplet arrays or square droplet arrays that we aim to construct. However, due to the interaction between droplets, these droplets do not form a stable structure. 


In summary, we systematically investigate the dynamics of 1D and 2D droplet arrays in two-dimensional rapidly rotating BECs. Our results demonstrate that the initial phase distribution is a key factor to determine the dynamics of droplet arrays. For individual droplets, the sheared velocity profile imposed by the phase plays an important role in the stability of the droplet. Furthermore, these conclusions can be directly generalized to moving droplet arrays such as oscillating and rotating. Based on these results, we propose a new scheme to design droplet arrays.

We further construct 2D droplet arrays with proper initial phase distributions. When the width or the distance in two parallel condensate strips increases, a crossover from the single droplet array to the double droplet array occurs in a large parameter region. Moreover, we demonstrate various 2D droplet configurations, such as multi-column droplet arrays, long-lived 2D structures achieved through subsystem partitioning, and other possible arrangements. Our findings indicate the scalability and tunability of phase-controlled droplet arrays and pave the way for the future study of novel dynamics in rotating BEC.

