%Local path planning aims to detect real-time changes in road conditions and dynamic obstacles, allowing the vehicle to adjust its driving path accordingly \cite{Yang:2022}. For instance, the path may be recalibrated to overcome situations such as approaching pedestrians, sudden speed changes in nearby vehicles, or unexpected road construction. Techniques for local path planning include geometry-based Pure Pursuit, model-based MPC (Model Predictive Control), and feedback error-based PID (Proportional-Integral-Derivative) Control. Among these, Pure Pursuit allows for simple and fast path tracking, while MPC predicts the vehicle's future states and generates optimized control signals for path adjustment \cite{Li:2023}. When these two algorithms are integrated, autonomous vehicles can navigate more efficiently and stably. Using MI in this integration allows for real-time data fusion, minimizing uncertainty, and optimizing path updates. In cases where dynamic obstacle avoidance is required, Pure Pursuit can handle simple path tracking to avoid obstacles. However, when combined with MPC, MI enables more precise obstacle avoidance in complex environments \cite{MacDonald:2016, He:2020}. This research \cite{MacDonald:2016} proposed that using MI significantly enhances the vehicle’s ability to avoid obstacles in complex and uncertain environments.

