\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:Preliminary}
This section introduces several preliminary definitions and results used in the analysis.

\subsection{Th Markov Chain for the Elitist EA}
Consider an EA designed to maximize a function \( f(x) \), where \( f(x) \) is defined over a finite set. The EA generates a sequence of solutions \( (X^{[t]})_{t \geq 0} \), where \( X^{[t]} \) represents the solution(s) at generation \( t \). We model the sequence  by a Markov chain \cite{he2003towards, he2016average}. Let \( X \) denote a state, \( S \) the state space, and \( S_{\mathrm{opt}} \) the set of optimal solutions. Throughout this paper, we assume that the Markov chain \( (X^{[t]})_{t \geq 0} \) satisfies three key properties.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \emph{Convergent}:  Starting from any $X\in S$, the chain can reach the optimal set $S_{\mathrm{opt}}$ with probability $1$.

    \item \emph{Homogeneous}: The transition probability $p(X,Y)$ from $X$ to $Y$ is independent on $t$.
    \item \emph{Elitist}:  Fitness values do not decrease: for any $t \ge 0$, $f(X^{[t+1]})\ge f(X^{[t]})$, where $f(X)$ is the fitness of $X$.
\end{enumerate} 

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Fitness Level Partitioning} 
A \emph{fitness level partition $(S_0, \ldots, S_K)$} \cite{wegener2003methods,sudholt2012new,doerr2024lower} is a partition of the state space $S$ into fitness levels according to the fitness from high to low  such that: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item  The highest level $S_0$ is the optimal set $S_{\mathrm{opt}}$.  
    \item For any pair of $X_k \in S_k$ and $X_{k+1} \in S_{k+1}$, the rank order holds: $f(X_k) > f(X_{k+1})$. 
\end{enumerate} 
%A state $X \in S_k$ is denoted by $X_k$. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:Jump} shows an example of a fitness level partition. % $\{S_0, \cdots, S_{12}\}$. 

\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{figureJump-1a.pdf}
        \caption{The x-axis represents the state and the y-axis represents the fitness value. Each arc is a transition. Two paths from $S_{12}$ to $S_1$ are highlighted.  }
    \label{fig:Jump}
\end{figure}

Given the Markov chain $(X^{[t]})_{t \ge 0}$, the transition probability from $X_k$ to $S_\ell$ (where $0 \le \ell \le K$) is 
\begin{equation}
    p(X_k, S_\ell) = \left\{
    \begin{array}{lll}
        0 &  \mbox{if } k < \ell, \\
      \in [0,1] & \mbox{{if }} k \ge \ell.
    \end{array}
    \right.
\end{equation}


Let $[i,j]$ denote the index set $\{i,i+1, \ldots, j-1, j \}$ and $S_{[i,j]}$ denote the union of levels $S_i \cup \cdots \cup S_{j}$.
The transition probability from $X_k$ to $S_{[i,j]}$  is denoted by $p(X_k,S_{[i,j]})$. 

In the fitness level method \cite{wegener2003methods,sudholt2012new,doerr2024lower}, a default assumption is that for any $k \ge 1$ and $X_k \in S_k$, the transition probability $p(X_k, S_{[0,k-1]})>0$. 
The  transition probability  from  $X^{[t]}=X_k$ to $X^{[t+1]}\in S_\ell $ conditional on   $X^{[t+1]} \notin S_k$ is  
\begin{align}
\label{equ:ConditionalProbability}
r(X_k,S_\ell)    =\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\frac{p(X_k, S_\ell)}{1-p(X_k, S_k)} &\mbox{if }\ell \neq k,\\
0 &\mbox{if } \ell =k.
\end{array}
\right.
\end{align}  


The minimum and maximum values of the transition probability $r(X_k,S_\ell)$ are expressed as follows: \begin{align*}
r_{{\scriptscriptstyle\min}}(X_k,S_\ell)&=\min_{X_k \in S_k}r(X_k,S_\ell),\\
r_{{\scriptscriptstyle\max}}(X_k,S_\ell) &=\max_{X_k \in S_k} r(X_k,S_\ell).
\end{align*}

Similar notation applies to the minimum and maximum values of the transition probability $p(X_k,S_\ell)$,  the hitting probability $h(X_k,S_\ell)$, and the mean hitting time $m(X_k,S_\ell)$ later. 

