Assume that the EA starts with an empty knapsack. According to feasibility rules, an infeasible solution will not be accepted because it is worse than the empty knapsack.
According to greedy repair, an infeasible solution will always be repaired to a feasible solution. Therefore, it is sufficient to view the state space as a set of feasible solutions. The fitness function is the objective function $f(x)$.

Six instances of the knapsack problem are presented in Table~\ref{tab:knapsack}. Each instance includes two high value items with large weights and $n-2$ low value items with small weights. Similar to TwoMax1 \cite{he2023drift},
Instance KP1 is easy to solve by the (1+1) EA. However, even a slight change in the capacity of the knapsack (Instance KP2), the value of Item 1 (Instance KP3), the weight of Item 2 (Instance KP4), or the value of Item 1 and the weight of Item 2 (Instance KP5) has the potential to transform an initially easy problem into a hard one. This alteration shifts the positions of the local and global optima, resulting in a ``wide gap'' between them \cite{he2003towards}. If there is no capacity constraint (Instance KP6), the knapsack problem degenerates into maximizing a linear function.

