\subsubsection{The (1+1) EA with Greedy Repair}  
Let $S_0$ denote the global optimal set $L_{(0,0;n-2)} \cup L_{(1,0;0)}$, and $S_1$ be the rest of feasible solutions.
According to the upper bound \eqref{equ:LinearUpperBound2} in Theorem \ref{the:TimeBounds},
the mean hitting time from any $x_1 \in S_1$  to $S_0$ is upper-bounded by
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\min_{x_1 \in S_1}p(x_1;S_0)}.
\end{align*}

For any $x_1=(0,b_2;k) \in S_1$, the probability of a mutation from $(0,b_2;k)$ to $(1,*;*)$ is $\frac{1}{n}$ (where $*$ represents an arbitrary value). After greedy repair by removing items with lower values, only item 1 remains and the solution becomes $(1,0;0)$. So the  probability   $p(x_1;S_0)$ is  at least
$\frac{1}{n}.$ 
Thus,  the mean hitting time from the empty knapsack to the global optimum 
\begin{align}
\label{instance1-c1}
    m(0,0;0)=O(n).
\end{align}

By comparing  \eqref{instance1-b3} and \eqref{instance1-c1}, we discover that the mean hitting time for the (1+1) EA with greedy repair is $O( {n^{-1}})$ of that for the (1+1) EA employing feasibility rules. For Instance KP1, greedy repair is better than feasibility rules.

