\begin{abstract}
  We present a novel perspective on the NFA canonization problem, which introduces intermediate minimization steps to reduce the exploration space on-the-fly.
  Essential to our approach are so-called \emph{equivalence registries} which manage information about equivalent states and allow for incorporating further optimization techniques such as convexity closures or simulation to boost performance.
  Due to the generality of our approach, these concepts can be embedded in classic subset construction or \citeauthor{brzozowski1962canonical}'s approach.
  We evaluate our approach on a set of real-world examples from automatic sequences and observe that we are able to improve especially worst-case scenarios.
  We implement our approach in an open-source library for users to experiment with.

  \keywords{Automata \and Canonization \and Minimization \and Determinization}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Finite state machines are foundational models of computation in computer science, where their simplicity and expressive power make them of both theoretical and practical interest.
Among the most prominent types are deterministic finite automata (DFAs) and non-deterministic finite automata (NFAs), which are extensively studied for their applications in various domains.
DFAs are often preferred over NFAs due to their efficiency in operations such as language equivalence and inclusion, universality, complementation, and other closure properties~\cite{hopcroft2001introduction, HolzerK11},
trace inclusion~\cite{van2008five}, and string matching~\cite{aho1975efficient, van2008five}.

\Citeauthor{RabinS59}~\cite{RabinS59} have shown that DFAs and NFAs are equally expressive by transforming NFAs into language-equivalent DFAs via \emph{subset} (or \emph{powerset}) \emph{construction}.
The resulting DFAs may be of exponential size compared to the original NFAs and may not be minimal.
The latter can be easily addressed via an additional minimization step~\cite{hopcroft1971n} which is often required if the resulting DFAs need to be processed further.
A minimal DFA is called \emph{canonical} since a minimal DFA is unique up to isomorphism~\cite{hopcroft2001introduction}.
Constructing the canonical DFA for a given NFA is called the \emph{canonization problem} \Cite{tabakov2005experimental, van2008five}.

The exponential blowup of canonization can be a challenge in practice.
However, many NFAs exhibit a notable \emph{compression ratio}, i.e., the ratio between the size of the determinized (via subset construction) DFA and its minimized version.
Real-world cases report compression ratios ranging from $30$ (\cite{fici2022properties}), to $2,500$ (\cite{Slavicki2021}), to $5$ million (\cite{Shallit2025b, Shallit2021}, and addenda for~\cite{mousavi2015mechanical}).
In such situations, materializing the full DFA is often the bottleneck of the canonization process.

For certain (NFA-related) problems there exists extensive research on limiting the impact of state blowup, e.g., by using \emph{antichains} for testing universality and language inclusion~\cite{de2006antichains,doyen2010antichain} or \emph{bisimulation up to congruence} for testing language equivalence~\cite{bonchi2013checking}.
See~\cite{chocholaty2024mata, garavel2022equivalence} for surveys on the current state of this work.
For the canonization problem, however, the construction of the full DFA is necessary.

In this paper, we present OTF -- an \emph{on-the-fly} approach for canonizing NFAs.
Instead of treating determinization and minimization as distinct phases of the canonization process, OTF interleaves these concepts by repeatedly minimizing the (partially) constructed DFA of subset construction.
Insights about equivalent states of the tentative DFA are then fed back to subset construction in order to reduce the state space requiring exploration.
This form of communication is formalized via \emph{equivalence registries} with which the process can be parameterized, giving OTF a framework character.
We present two exemplary implementations of equivalence registries.
In our evaluation, we show that OTF is able to improve the performance of canonization for complex real-world NFAs, while remaining competitive for simple systems.
We implement these concepts in a public tool\footnote{\url{https://github.com/jn1z/OTF}} for users to experiment with.

The idea of exploiting equivalencies to reduce state space appears in various automaton-related fields of research such as
linear integer arithmetic~\cite{HabermehlHHHL24} and WS1S formulae~\cite{FiedorHLV15}.
However, these approaches typically exploit some domain-specific knowledge of their use-cases.
In contrast, OTF processes generic NFAs, allowing for a flexible application.
Additionally, by processing NFAs directly, OTF is compatible with other directions of NFA minimization including (bi-) simulation reduction, transition pruning, and transition saturation~\cite{ilie2003follow,champarnaud2004nfa,ilie2004nfa, clemente2019efficient}.

A noteworthy alternative to classical NFA canonization is \citeauthor{brzozowski1962canonical}'s algorithm~\cite{brzozowski1962canonical} which first determinizes (e.g., via subset construction) the reversed NFA, then reverses the constructed DFA, and finally determinizes it again.
This method canonizes the original NFA without requiring an additional minimization step and can outperform classic subset construction in some cases.
Since it relies only on determinization, it is compatible with OTF and is included in our evaluation.
Finally, \Cite{van2008five} considers the potential of compression ratio by generalizing subset construction although its performance is not compelling.

