\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:prelminaries}

This section introduces basic notations and concepts used throughout the paper.

\subsection{Finite Automata}

Let $\Sigma$ denote a finite, non-empty set of input symbols and $\Sigma^*$ denote the set of finite \emph{words} over $\Sigma$ with $\varepsilon$ denoting the \emph{empty word}.

\begin{definition}[Finite State Acceptor]
  A \emph{finite state acceptor} (FSA) is a tuple ${A = (S, \Sigma, \delta, S_0, F)}$ such that
  \begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item $S$ denotes a finite, non-empty set of states,
    \item $\Sigma$ denotes a finite, non-empty set of input symbols,
    \item $\delta \subseteq S \times \Sigma \times S$ denotes a labeled transition relation,
    \item $S_0 \subseteq S$ denotes a set of initial states,
    \item and $F \subseteq S$ denotes a set of final or accepting states.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Let $2^X$ denote the set of subsets of a set $X$.
By abuse of notation, we may interpret $\delta$ as a function $S \times \Sigma \mapsto 2^S$ such that $\delta(s, i) = \{t \in S \mid (s, i, t) \in \delta \}$.
We generalize this view to sets of states and words such that ${\delta(Q, i) = \bigcup_{q \in Q} \delta(q, i)}$ and ${\delta(s, \varepsilon) = s, \delta(s, iw) = \delta(\delta(s, i), w)}$ for ${Q \subseteq S, s \in S, i \in \Sigma, w \in \Sigma^*}$.
In the following, we may refer to a set of states $Q$ as a \emph{metastate}.

A word $w$ is \emph{accepted} by a state ${s \in S}$ iff ${\delta(s, w) \cap F \neq \emptyset}$.
The \emph{language} of a state $s \in S$, $L(s)$, is defined as set of accepted words of $s$.
The language of a metastate $Q$ is defined as ${L(Q) = \bigcup_{q \in Q} L(q)}$.
The language of an FSA $A$ is defined as ${L(A) = L(S_0)}$.
Two FSAs, $A$ and $B$, are said to be \emph{language equivalent} iff ${L(A) = L(B)}$.

We define the \emph{reversed} automaton of $A$ as the FSA ${A^R = (S, \Sigma, \delta^{-1}, F, S_0)}$ where $\delta^{-1}$ denotes the inverse transition relation.%
\footnote{While typically defined on binary relations, the inverse can be easily generalized to higher dimensional tuples: ${(a, b ,c)^{-1} = (c, b, a)}$.}
The reversed language $L(A^R)$ is the language accepted by the reversed automaton $A^R$.

We call $A$ a \emph{deterministic} finite state acc`     eptor (DFA) if and only if ${\vert S_0 \vert = 1}$ and ${\vert \delta(s, i) \vert \leq 1}$ for all ${s \in S, i \in \Sigma}$.
Otherwise, $A$ may be referred to as a \emph{non-deterministic} finite state acceptor (NFA).

\subsection{Simulation and Bisimulation}

For reasoning about the behavioral relationships between states of an FSA, there exist various notions of equivalence.
A popular one is (bi-) simulation~\cite{milner89}.

\begin{definition}[Simulation]
  Let $A$ be an FSA\@.
  A binary relation $R \subseteq S \times S$ is called a \emph{simulation relation} iff ${(x,y) \in R}$ implies
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $x \in F \Leftrightarrow y \in F$ and
    \item $\forall i \in \Sigma\colon (x, i, x') \in \delta \Rightarrow (\exists y' \in S\colon (y, i, y') \in \delta \land (x', y') \in R)$.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bisimulation]
  Let $A$ be an FSA\@.
  A binary relation $R \subseteq S \times S$ is called a \emph{bisimulation relation} iff $R$ and $R^{-1}$ are simulation relations for $A$.
\end{definition}

Note that bisimulation is stricter than simulation equivalence, as it requires the inverse of the \emph{same} relation to also be a simulation.

The coarsest simulation and bisimulation relations, known as \emph{similarity} and \emph{bisimilarity}, can be computed in $\mathcal{O}(|\delta| \cdot |S|)$ time~\cite{ranzato2007new} and $\mathcal{O}(|\delta| \cdot \log |S|)$ time~\cite{valmari2009bisimilarity}, respectively.
It is well known that simulation implies finite trace (i.e., language) inclusion~\cite{clemente2019efficient, van2008five, glabbeek1990linear} and bisimulation implies finite trace (i.e., language) equivalence~\cite{glabbeek1990linear}.
Thus, bisimulation or simulation equivalences can be used to quotient NFAs, which is often done to reduce the size of NFAs before determinizing.
Note that this step is only relevant for non-deterministic systems since for DFAs, minimization and bisimulation quotienting coincide~\cite{bonchi2013checking}.

\subsection{Semi-lattice, Convexity, Antichain}
\label{subsec:lattice}

Let $X$ denote a finite set.
A \emph{semi-lattice} ${(X, \circ)}$ is an algebraic structure that consists of $X$ and a binary operation ${\circ : X \times X \rightarrow X}$ which is associative, commutative, and idempotent.
Let $(X, \circ)$ be a semi-lattice and ${M \subseteq X}$.
We call $(M, \circ)$ a (semi-) \emph{sub-lattice} of $(X, \circ)$ iff $(M, \circ)$ is a well-defined semi-lattice itself.
We call a semi-lattice $(M, \circ)$ \emph{convex} iff ${\{y \in X \mid x, z \in M, x \preceq y \preceq z\} \subseteq M}$.
Here, $\prec$ denotes the preorder related to the respective (join/meet) operator $\circ$.
Let ${L = (M, \circ)}$ be a semi-lattice.
The \emph{convexity closure} of $L$ is obtained by adding all missing elements to $L$ in order to make it convex.

In this paper, we mainly consider variations of the well-known powerset lattice ${(2^X, \cup, \cap)}$.
In particular, ${(2^X, \cup)}$ is a join-semi-lattice.
Furthermore, let $X = \{1, \ldots, 5\}, A = \{1,2\}, B = \{3,4\}$.
Then $L_1 = {(\{A, B, A \cup B\}, \cup)}$ is a \mbox{(semi-)} sub-lattice of $(2^X, \cup)$ and the convexity closure of $L_1$ is given by \[(\{1,2\}, \{3,4\}, \{1,2,3\}, \{1,2,4\}, \{2,3,4\}, \{1,2,3,4\}\}, \cup).\]

Testing whether an element is contained in a convex (join-) semi-lattice $L$ can be efficiently implemented by testing whether it is covered from above by $L$'s greatest element and covered from below by any of $L$'s minimal elements.
Let $L = (X, \cup)$ denote a (join-) semi-lattice.
The \emph{greatest element} of $L$ is given by ${\bigcup L = \bigcup_{x \in X} x}$.
An element ${x \in X}$ is called \emph{minimal} iff ${\nexists y \in X\colon y \subset x}$.
Here, $\subset$ is the related preorder of the join operator ($\cup$) without equality.
For $L_1$, the greatest element is $\{1,2,3,4\}$ and the minimal elements are $\{1,2\}$ and $\{3,4\}$.
The element $\{1,2,3\}$ is contained in the convexity closure of $L_1$ because ${\{1,2\} \subseteq \{1,2,3\}}$ and ${\{1,2,3\} \subseteq \{1,2,3,4\}}$.
It is worth noting that the minimal elements of $L$ form an antichain, i.e., a set of pairwise incomparable (in the sense of $\prec$) elements, and can be efficiently stored in a list-like structure.

\section{The OTF Framework}
\label{sec:otf}

This section presents how we integrate the OTF concept into the determinization process.
Intuitively, our approach is similar to classic subset construction with two major modifications.

First, the NFA state-space exploration may be repeatedly interrupted by a \emph{threshold} predicate.
During this interrupt, the partially constructed DFA is minimized by an efficient algorithm such as~\cite{hopcroft1971n}.
On one hand, this reduces the size of the intermediate DFA model.
On the other, the information about equivalent DFA states may be used to optimize the remaining exploration of the NFA state-space (see below).

Second, the mapping between NFA metastates and DFA states is handled by an \emph{equivalence registry} supporting the following operations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item GET$(Q)$ returns a metastate identifier with the same language as metastate $Q$, or undefined if this behavior has not been previously observed.
  \item PUT$(Q,x)$ associates the behavior of metastate $Q$ with identifier $x$.
  \item UNIFY$(x_1, x_2)$ marks metastates $x_1$ and $x_2$ as (language) equivalent.
\end{itemize}
Looking up identifiers \emph{modulo language-equivalence} has a huge potential to reduce the metastate space that needs to be explored, thus improving determinization performance.

We design OTF as a \emph{framework} by allowing the threshold and equivalence registry to be parameters to this process.
In practice, this allows users to construct highly customizable canonization processes specifically tailored to their use-cases.

\subsection{Algorithmic Sketch}

\cref{alg:otf} formalizes and outlines a possible implementation.
While our actual implementation includes additional technical optimizations (e.g., batch calls, buffer re-use), we focus here on the general concepts.

\begin{algorithm}
  \caption{On-The-Fly Determinization}
  \label{alg:otf}
  \input{includes//otf}
\end{algorithm}

The algorithm starts by initializing a counter used to identify states of the resulting DFA, and creates the corresponding state sets (regular, initial, and final).
The equivalence registry and exploration queue are then initialized with information about the initial NFA states.
We choose a last-in-first-out queue (stack) which traverses the NFA state-space depth-first.
The set $E'$ tracks the fully explored states of the final DFA $A'$.

The main exploration loop starts at \cref{line:expl}.
The current metastate $C$ is removed from the queue and its representative $c'$ is retrieved from the registry $R$.
Every element in $Q$ has a representative in $R$, ensuring $c'$ is always defined.
For each input symbol, the next metastate $N$ is computed and its representative $n'$ is looked up.
If $n'$ is not found, a new state is added to $A'$ by incrementing the counter and updating the relevant variables.
The registry is then updated and $N$ is queued for future exploration.
Afterward, the respective transition can be set.
Once all outgoing transitions of $c'$ are set, $c'$ is marked as explored.

In \cref{line:otf}, the \textsc{threshold} predicate determines if minimization occurs.
If so, an associative array $\mathit{sig}$ is built, storing each state's $\mathit{signature}$ -- a Boolean value for explored states' acceptance or a unique numerical identifier for unexplored states.
$A'$ is then minimized using $\mathit{sig}$ as the initial partition.
For convenience, we assume that \textsc{minimize} mutates $A'$ directly, eliminating the need for explicit updates to related sets and functions.
Additionally, \textsc{minimize} returns pairs of equivalent states, which are forwarded to the registry to refine future look-ups (cf. \cref{sec:registry}).
The exploration loop then proceeds with the next metastate.

Once the exploration queue is empty, the final iteration of $A'$ is returned.
While $A'$ may be minimized during exploration, the threshold predicate might not trigger in the last iteration.
Thus, a final minimization is necessary for canonizing $A'$.

\subsection{Termination and Correctness}

With a constant \textbf{false} predicate and an identity-based registry that only retrieves (GET) or stores (PUT) identifiers, \cref{alg:otf} coincides with classic subset construction.
Thus, we omit general discussions about termination and correctness, as they follow directly from the original algorithm.
Instead, we focus on how our modifications preserve these properties.

The first modification relaxes GET to return representatives of language-equivalent metastates instead of previously associated ones.
Once classic subset construction terminates, these metastates correspond to equivalent DFA states, which can be permuted or merged without affecting the language of the DFA\@.
Allowing these transformations earlier (during lookup) only shortcuts these steps while preserving language equivalence.

The second modification introduces intermediate minimizations, where unexplored states are assigned unique blocks in the initial partition, ensuring they remain distinct until their behavior is fully determined.
Consequently, minimization never merges states with incomplete information.
Only explored states with identical acceptance and identical successors are merged, meaning the final DFA remains equivalent to its non-minimized counterpart.
Thus, intermediate minimizations do not alter the DFA’s language, maintaining parity with classic subset construction.

\section{Equivalence Registries}
\label{sec:registry}

The performance of OTF largely depends on the chosen equivalence registry.
Intelligently looking up and effectively unifying language-equivalent metastates can drastically mitigate the blow-up of the exploration space, a key limitation of classic subset construction.
We continue with presenting two implementations that performed well in our evaluation.

\subsection{CCL -- Convexity Closure Lattice}
\label{subsec:ccl}

The CCL registry uses the concept of convexity closure of lattices.
It is based on an observation of \citeauthor{bonchi2013checking}~\cite{bonchi2013checking} that for metastates $A, B$ of an NFA: \[L(A \cup B) = L(A) \cup L(B).\]
Exploiting this property further, we can conclude for ${L(A) = L(B)}$ that \[L(A \cup B) = L(A) \cup L(B) = L(A) = L(B).\]
This relationship is not limited to disjoint metastates.
Consider the situation where ${L(A) = L(A \cup B)}$ and ${A \subseteq A \cup B}$.
This means that no state in ${B \setminus A}$ impacts the language of either $L(A)$ or $L(A \cup B)$.
In particular, for every $X$ such that ${A \subseteq X \subseteq A \cup B}$, we have \[L(A) = L(X) = L(A \cup B).\]
The same reasoning applies to ${L(B) = L(A \cup B)}$, placing us in a scenario similar to \cref{subsec:lattice}: detecting equivalence between two metastates allows one to construct a (join-semi-) lattice $L$ with minimal elements $A,B$ and greatest element ${A \cup B}$.
$L$ itself forms a sub-lattice for the powerset lattice of the global metastate space.
Due to these properties, language equivalence holds for all elements in the convexity closure of $L$.
Thus, recognizing equivalence between two metastates $A$, $B$ provides insight into many (potentially unobserved) metastates.

The registry manages a distinct lattice for each DFA state, covering its language-equivalent metastates.
Initially, when a new metastate is registered (PUT), a singleton lattice is stored.
When unifying two states (UNIFY), their associated lattices $L_1,L_2$ must be merged.
If both are singletons, the process is straightforward.
However, if either has undergone previous merges, we recursively apply convexity closure: since all states covered by $L_1,L_2$ are language-equivalent, we select their greatest elements as representatives and apply the same steps as before.
The greatest element of the new lattice $L'$ is the union of the greatest elements of $L_1,L_2$.
Since union is the join operator, $L'$ covers all elements covered by $L_1,L_2$ from above.
The minimal elements of $L'$ are constructed from the union of minimal elements $M_1,M_2$ of $L_1,L_2$, respectively.
Here, elements in $M_1,M_2$ may be in a subset relation which would violate minimality in $L'$.
To tackle this issue, we simply filter out these elements which requires at most $\mathcal{O}(|M_1||M_2|)$ subset comparisons.

For GET operations, recall from \cref{subsec:lattice} that the convexity closure of a lattice can be efficiently stored using its antichain of minimal elements and greatest element.
Thus, the registry first scans for eligible lattices whose greatest element is a superset of $S$.
Then, for each candidate, the respective antichain is searched for elements that are a subset of $S$.
If such a candidate is found, the associated representative is returned.

This description focuses on the functional view of the registry, though many implementation optimizations exist.
For example, singleton lattices can be retrieved via a faster hash-based data structure rather than the general lookup logic.
Also, subset and superset checks can be accelerated using inverse indices and bit-vector operations: see~\cite{luo2015designing} for an involved discussion of options.
We omit specifics for clarity, instead referring to our publicly available implementation.

\subsection{CCLS -- CCL with Similarity}
\label{subsec:ccls}

The core mechanism of the CCL registry is the intermediate minimization that establishes equivalencies between NFA metastates via UNIFY calls.
Thus, the registry can typically reduce the search space only after minimization.
Using a similar idea as~\cite{abdulla2010simulation,clemente2019efficient,van2008five}, the CCLS registry refines the CCL approach by introducing preprocessing to compute the similarity relation on the original NFA, then exploiting state-level relations to accelerate metastate equivalence detection, potentially even before UNIFY is invoked.

Let $a,b$ be NFA states\@.
If $a$ \emph{simulates} $b$, then ${L(b) \subseteq L(a)}$ which implies ${L(\{a,b\}) = L(\{a\})}$.
In metastate terms, we can replace $\{a,b\}$ with $\{a\}$ (pruning) and $\{a\}$ with $\{a,b\}$ (saturating).
This principle extends to supersets as well, e.g., $\{a,b,c\}$ can be pruned to $\{a,c\}$ and $\{a,c\}$ can be saturated to $\{a,b,c\}$.

The CCLS registry leverages this in two ways.
GET$(S)$ first prunes $S$ into $S_p$ using similarity information and then finds a lattice covering $S_p$, as in the CCL registry.
PUT$(S, x)$ first prunes and saturates $S$ into $S_p$ and $S_s$: if ${S_p \neq S_s}$, instead of storing a singleton lattice like CCL, it creates a \enquote{complex} lattice with $S_p$ as a single minimal element and $S_s$ as the greatest element.
Thus, each lattice may cover a larger metastate space \enquote{for free}, further reducing the search space.

The effectiveness of the CCLS registry depends on the use case.
Computing the simulation preorder can be prohibitively expensive for large or dense NFAs, so the benefit of precomputing simulation relations must outweigh the cost.
On an implementation level, we preprocess the NFA's initial and final states following techniques from~\cite{clemente2019efficient}.
Additionally, since the CCLS registry requires similarity information at construction time, we compute it before running OTF\@.
As a result, we can further quotient the system via simulation equivalence.
This also allows us to avoid challenges such as cyclic subset inclusions and tie-breaking during metastate selection~\cite{van2008five}.

\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

This section compares the performance of OTF with classic NFA canonization algorithms.
For the evaluation, we focus on practical examples from the field of automatic sequences.
In particular, we analyze various case-studies of the tool Walnut~\cite{mousavi2016automatic} and the automaton models it generates.
We refer the interested reader to \cref{sec:app} for further benchmarks on synthetic systems.
All benchmarks and results are open-source\footnote{\url{https://github.com/jn1z/OTF_benchmarks}} for readers to replicate.

We continue with a brief overview of the considered systems, the configuration of the benchmark, and an evaluation of the results.

\subsection{Systems}
\label{subsec:sys}

The automata arose as intermediate calculations in Büchi-based arithmetic problems using the Walnut tool, from~\cite{bell2020additive, bosma2025using, currie2023properties, fici2022properties, meleshko2023pseudoperiodic, mignoty2024automatic, mousavi2015mechanical, schaeffer2024first, Shallit2021, Shallit2024, Shallit2025, shallit2023automatic, shallit2024power, shallit2019circular} and personal communications with Jeffrey Shallit.

To normalize the input automata we further applied some typical redundancy elimination techniques in a pre-processing step,
trimming each automaton (i.e., removing non-accessible and non-co-accessible states) and quotienting according to the concerned configuration (see below) either by bisimulation~\cite{valmari2009bisimilarity} or simulation-equivalence~\cite{clemente2019efficient,ranzato2007new}.
We found the LIGHT- and HEAVY- reduction methods from~\cite{clemente2019efficient} to be impractical in our benchmarks due to their worst-case performance on NFAs.
However, we did include their preprocessing steps of modifying initial and final states to find more simulation relations.

\subsection{Configurations}
\label{subsec:conf}

We evaluated the following algorithms and OTF configurations:

\begin{description}
  \item[OTF] describes the OTF-based canonization process using the CCL equivalence registry presented in \cref{subsec:ccl}.
  \item[OTF-S] describes the OTF-based canonization process using the CCLS equivalence registry presented in\cref{subsec:ccls}.
  \item[SC] describes the canonization process via classic subset construction.
  \item[SC-S] describes a variation of SC with pruning and saturation.
  Internally, we use the OTF-S implementation with a constant \textbf{false} predicate, i.e., no intermediate minimizations.
  This closely resembles SUBSET(close$_{\simpreorder}$) from~\cite{van2008five}.
  \item[BRZ] describes \citeauthor{brzozowski1962canonical}'s~\cite{brzozowski1962canonical} canonization algorithm.
  \item[BRZ-S] describes a variation of BRZ which uses SC-S for the first determinization phase.
  Recall that BRZ constructs canonical automata without any minimization, i.e., the second phase does not create any equivalent states.
  As a result, we use plain SC for the second phase in this configuration.
  \item[BRZ-OTF] describes a variant of BRZ that uses OTF for the first phase.
  \item[BRZ-OTF-S] describes a variant of BRZ that uses OTF-S for the first phase.
\end{description}

All configurations use Hopcroft's algorithm~\cite{hopcroft1971n} to minimize either the intermediate automata (OTF) or the final automaton (OTF, SC).
OTF and BRZ-OTF configurations use an \textit{adaptive threshold}, which is weighted based upon the size of the intermediate automaton, and previous performance of minimization.
This prevents worst-case behavior where time-consuming minimizations are applied repeatedly for little gain.

\subsection{Evaluation}

The benchmarks were executed on an AMD EPYC 7763 CPU (2.45-3.5GHz) running a JDK 17.0.14 with 1.8TB assigned memory.
Note that the memory was mainly needed to run multiple benchmarks in parallel and does not indicate the actual memory requirements of our approach.
In fact, we have often seen a much lower memory consumption compared to the alternatives.
For each configuration, we consider the total runtime of the canonization process which includes the time for pre-processing steps such as computing (bi-) similarity.
The full data is available in the benchmark repository.

\Cref{fig:walnut-cactus} presents a survival plot of the results.
The x-axis shows the time required for canonization in base 10 where we aborted a process after a timeout of one hour.
The y-axis shows the number of successfully canonized systems out of the 42 systems in total.
All \textbf{-S}-based configurations are indicated by dashed lines (sim = true).

\begin{figure}[tp]%
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{images/walnutSurv}%
  \caption{Survival plot of the Walnut examples.}%
  \label{fig:walnut-cactus}%
\end{figure}

The results allow for analyses in different directions.
When comparing BRZ-based configurations with non-BRZ-based ones, we see that especially for smaller / faster systems, BRZ-based configurations outperform their competitors as more systems are canonized.
However, it seems that these approaches hit a \enquote{wall} after which only very little progress can be observed (see after $10$ and $100$ seconds).
Here, only the OTF-based variation of BRZ is able to break this pattern and eventually catch up with the total number of canonized systems.
In contrast, the non-BRZ-based configurations show a more continuous progression.

Regarding the impact of simulation, we see that the BRZ-based configurations are largely unaffected as their performance remains relatively similar.
Only for systems near the timeout threshold, we can see that omitting simulation information may actually improve performance.
The full data shows that the computation of the simulation relation only requires a few seconds so the time is lost in the CCLS implementation.
It seems that the overhead of pruning and saturating the queried metastates is greater than the saved exploration time of the reversed automata.
In contrast, the non-BRZ-based approaches seem to benefit from including the simulation information.
The plain subset construction experiences a small performance boost for fast(er) systems but eventually meets up with its non-simulated version so that no continuing trend is observable.
Only OTF is able to significantly reduce canonization time and maintain its advantage over the other configurations.
In particular, extrapolating the results for large systems from~\cite{shallit2024power}, OTF-variant algorithms performed over $100$ times better.

Regarding systems that timed out, they seem to have a comparatively low compression ratio which results in high DFA state counts.
Initially, we ran the benchmarks without any timeout and noticed that the process would run out of memory, affecting further data collection.
In particular, the benchmark includes systems such as \texttt{tribpseudo3} (\enquote{Open Problem 30} in~\cite{meleshko2023pseudoperiodic}) which has not been successfully canonized yet.

In summary, we can see that OTF does introduce overhead which is especially notable for systems whose canonization process is fast to begin with.
However, the longer this process needs ($>100$ seconds) the more promising the results of OTF become.
Framing these observations in terms of absolute time consumption, we argue that OTF helps especially when dealing with complex systems.
In contrast, the overhead for smaller / faster systems ($<10$ seconds) is often negligible in practice.

\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{sec:fut}

In this paper, we present OTF -- a framework for NFA canonization.
OTF combines on-the-fly minimization with dynamic equivalence registries for which we present two possible implementations.
Our performance evaluation shows that for NFAs with \enquote{structure}, the discussed OTF configurations outperform classic subset construction and \citeauthor{brzozowski1962canonical}'s algorithm for complex systems while being competitive for the remaining ones.
Looking forward, there exist several directions for extension.

One obvious direction deals with exploring further implementations of equivalence registries.
Full set-rewriting as with HKC~\cite{bonchi2013checking} can capture additional relations between equivalence classes, thus reducing exploration space even more.
However, this was slower in our testing (see~\cite{fu2017equivalence} for more information).
This appeared to be due to high-cardinality equivalences, which are both slow and unlikely to match a given metastate.
There is the possibility of a hybridized HKC approach that is limited to smaller cardinalities, or alternatively, a hierarchical approach.
These ideas touch on the vast Formal Concept Analysis literature~\cite{bertet2018lattices}.

Furthermore, states and transitions can be represented as a binary decision diagram~\cite{chocholaty2024mata,touzeau2019fast}, potentially combining with a semi-symbolic approach~\cite{de2006antichains}.
This may be helpful in cases where systems have large alphabets~\cite{fu2017equivalence}.

Finally, we note an equivalence between the steps of subset construction and a sequence of mutated NFAs, either built directly from the original NFA states, or from a more efficient \emph{cover} including new DFA metastates.
The minimization steps of OTF could be applied to a mutated NFA, potentially finding more equivalences, but requiring a more involved minimization algorithm~\cite{valmari2009bisimilarity}.
A mutated NFA also allows for dynamically switching determinization strategies, which can break out of local minima, i.e., particularly difficult determinization sections.

