\appendix
\section{Additional Benchmark Results}
\label{sec:app}

In the following, we discuss the performance of OTF for another class of automata.
The full benchmark and results are public\footnote{\url{https://github.com/jn1z/OTF_benchmarks}} as well.

\subsection{Iterated Language Projection of Random Systems}

\emph{Language projection} is a fundamental operation in formal language theory.
Given a language ${L \subseteq \Sigma^*}$ over an alphabet $\Sigma$ and an onto function ${\pi : \Sigma \to \Gamma \cup \{\tau\}}$ where $\tau$ denotes a special symbol for \enquote{quiescence}, the projected language ${\pi(L)}$ is obtained by applying $\pi$ to each symbol of every word in $L$.
In particular, symbols mapped to $\tau$ are effectively \enquote{hidden} or \enquote{deleted} which can cause transitions in the corresponding automaton to merge.
When applied to a DFA, this operation usually introduces non-determinism and transforms the given DFA into an NFA\@.

A key application of this concept arises in \emph{Presburger quantifier elimination}~\cite{Buchi1960,haase2018survival}.
While language projection imposes some structure already, real-world scenarios typically involve multiple or \emph{iterated} projection steps in which several intermediate automata are combined via production constructions.
To analyze the performance of OTF in these situations, this benchmark looks at the canonization of repeatedly language projected products of random DFAs.

\subsubsection{Systems}

Each benchmark run is parameterized by a random seed ${r \in \{0, \ldots, 99\}}$, an alphabet size ${s \in \{8, 16, 32\}}$, and a compression factor ${\mathit{cf} \in \{1.3,1.7\}}$.
First, for a given seed, two random DFAs with four states and $s$ input symbols are generated.
These DFAs are then projected onto an NFA with $\frac{s}{cf}$ input symbols such that the preimage partition classes of the projection are equally sized.
Then the symmetric difference of the two NFA language is constructed via an intermediate determinization step and a product automaton construction using the logical exclusive-or as state acceptance.
Finally, the resulting DFA is projected again using the similar projection steps.

While four states for the initial NFAs seem relatively low, even small changes to this number result in either trivial or practically infeasible systems.
In contrast, controlling the final sizes via the number of input symbols as well as the compression factor allows for a much more fine-grained control over the resulting systems.

\subsubsection{Configurations}

We use the same configurations as described in \cref{subsec:conf}.

\subsubsection{Evaluation}

\Cref{fig:proj-comb} shows two survival plots of the benchmark results, comparing the elapsed runtime against the number of successfully canonized systems.
The plots are split according to the compression factor but otherwise aggregate all data over the random seeds and alphabet sizes.
Each benchmark was configured to timeout after ten minutes.

\begin{figure}[tp]
  \includegraphics[page=1,width=\textwidth]{images/projCombSurv}
  \includegraphics[page=2,width=\textwidth]{images/projCombSurv}
  \caption{Survival plot of the iterated language projection examples.}
  \label{fig:proj-comb}
\end{figure}

Beginning with the results for a compression factor of $1.3$ (upper half), the plot only contains data for OTF and subset construction.
All BRZ-based configurations ran into memory problems which prevented a reliable extraction of timing results.
We observed that the issues often occurred during construction of the reversed automata when determining successor states.
This suggests to us that the constructed automata are very \enquote{join-heavy}, i.e., reversing the adjacency structure results in big sets of successor states, which puts BRZ at a disadvantage by design.

For the available data, two interesting properties can be observed.
The configurations involving simulation outperform the ones that do not and subset construction outperforms OTF\@.
Analyzing these effects simultaneously highlights a central property: OTF mainly benefits from equivalences detected during minimization.
The fact that subset construction (which does not perform any minimizations) outperforms OTF indicates that the intermediate minimizations are not able to significantly reduce the search space but instead, the management of equivalence registries introduces additional overhead.
At the same time, the systems do compress as indicated by the speedup obtained when including simulation information.
Still, the data shows that OTF (through the CCLS registry) is able to leverage additional knowledge such as similarity and remains relatively competitive with subset construction, even in this disadvantageous use-case.

For systems with a more aggressive compression factor of $1.7$ (lower half), we can see that the BRZ-based configurations are able to terminate again.
In fact, they even outperform the non-BRZ-based configurations by a small margin up until the very last few systems, which aligns with the trends observed in \cref{fig:walnut-cactus}.
Another interesting point is that the impact of the simulation information seems to have an inverted effect on the BRZ-based configurations as the \enquote{non-sim} configurations outperform the \enquote{sim}-based ones.
The performance of BRZ and BRZ-OTF is nearly identical because due to the structure (size) of the systems, only very few (often zero) intermediate minimizations are performed.

In summary, this benchmark gives an indication of how different system structures can positively or negatively impact canonization performance, which is another interesting direction for future research.
We see OTF as an enabler for incorporating several useful heuristics through its equivalence registries which allow one to adapt to various environments and external factors.

