\section{Problems and main results}
\label{sec:problems}
This work aims to provide a theoretical explanation for the high learning accuracy observed in the Gröbner basis computation experiments over the field of rational numbers, as reported in \cite{KIKVY24}. In particular, we demonstrate that the training data generated by the algorithm satisfies a certain notion of density, which ensures a rich and diverse set of examples conducive to effective learning. Our result suggests that the empirical success in the rational setting is not incidental but supported by an underlying mathematical structure. While models perform less accurately over finite fields, possibly due to the absence of such density, our findings offer a structural perspective that can guide future theoretical investigations; see also Remark \ref{rem:coefficient fields}.
\subsection{A problem concerning datasets}
