Upon cooling from higher temperatures, bulk CrI\textsubscript{3} undergoes a structural transition from the high-temperature monoclinic (C/2m) to the low-temperature rhombohedral ($\mathrm{R\bar{3}}$) phase at $T_S=\SI{210}{\K}$ \cite{Meseguer2021}. The rhombohedral CrI\textsubscript{3} structure displays ferromagnetic (FM) order below its Curie temperature of $T_C=\SI{61}{\K}$. The appearance of both magnetic and structural transitions is commonplace among vdW ferromagnets, due to the interplay between crystalline anisotropy and magnetic order.

The rhombohedral crystal structure \cite{Momma2011}, bulk and surface Brillouin zone (BZ), as well as the simple scheme of nominal Cr 3\textit{d} orbital filling are depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig1}a-c. Firstly, we consider ARPES data obtained on CrI\textsubscript{3} at $T=\SI{300}{\K}$, probing valence band (VB) states. The isoenergetic surface and the (\textit{E}, \textit{k}) spectra along the high symmetry directions in the surface BZ are presented in Fig. \ref{fig:Fig1}d-e. Darker color indicates higher electron count. 
The energy axis in the (\textit{E}, \textit{k}) spectra has been aligned such that the valence band maximum (VBM) corresponds to zero energy. The measurements are consistent with previous results \cite{DeVita2022}: the VBM is dominated by strongly dispersive states, displaying a slight trigonal warping (Fig. \ref{fig:Fig1}d), whereas at higher binding energies (BEs) from \SIrange{-1}{-2}{\eV} the electronic states appear more localized.

To complement experimental results, we model the high-temperature electronic structure using DFT. Due to the vdW nature of bulk CrI\textsubscript{3} and the presence of spin-orbit-coupling(SOC)-induced anisotropy, the band structure evolution across the magnetic phase transition is dominated by intralayer interactions, with interlayer effects playing a secondary role. Therefore, we employed the monolayer CrI\textsubscript{3} as a simplified model for the system under investigation and performed non-collinear DFT calculations with SOC included.   
%
\begin{comment}
We used the PBEsol exchange-correlation functional \cite{Perdew2008Apr}, following the work of Haddadi \textit{et al.}, which demonstrates that PBEsol provides a more accurate description of the electronic bands of CrI\textsubscript{3} compared to the PBE functional \cite{Haddadi2024Jan}. Some kind of on-site energy correction is expected in this system, as the insulating ground state despite the partially filled Cr 3\textit{d} orbital indicates a Mott-Hubbard-driven stabilization of a bandgap \cite{Webster2018}. Therefore, we used the DFT+\textit{U} approach as introduced by Liechtenstein \textit{et al.} \cite{Liechtenstein1995} with Hubbard $U=\SI{1}{\eV}$ and Hund $J=\SI{0.9}{\eV}$. This approach allows us to strike a balance between the findings of Stavroupolous \textit{et al.} \cite{Stavropoulos2021}, who argue that a finite Hund coupling $J$ is essential for stabilizing ferromagnetic order in CrI\textsubscript{3}, and the results of Haddadi \textit{et al.} \cite{Haddadi2024Jan}, which demonstrate that the PBEsol functional with a small Hubbard $U$ provides good agreement overall with experimental electronic structure data. As argued in Ref.~\cite{Haddadi2024Jan}, the agreement further improves when distinct Hubbard \textit{U} parameters are used for the spin-up and spin-down channels, though we ultimately decided to use a single \textit{U} for both channels.

Since the distinction between the ferromagnetic (FM) and paramagnetic (PM) states is central to this work, a detailed explanation of modeling the magnetic order in CrI\textsubscript{3} is necessary. The FM state was modeled with Cr magnetic moments of $3 \, \mu_{\rm B}$ aligned out-of-plane and parallel to each other. Spin-orbit coupling (SOC) was included, and non-collinear DFT calculations were performed. In contrast, the PM state was modeled using the \textit{special quasi-random structures} (SQS) approach for binary alloys~\cite{Zunger1990Jul}, where a $3 \times 3 \times 1$ supercell containing 18 Cr atoms was constructed and the {\sc icet} tool~\cite{Angqvist2019Jul} was used to distribute 9 spin-up and 9 spin-down out-of-plane Cr moments in a quasi-random configuration, mimicking a binary alloy on a honeycomb lattice. This ensures zero net magnetization while suppressing long-range order, following a similar approach to Ref.~\cite{Chen2020Dec}.
It should be noted that DFT is a zero-temperature theory, which inherently limits its ability to fully describe the physical properties of the system in the real PM state at higher temperatures. In this regard, the DFT-simulated PM state represents an approximation where Cr magnetic moments retain their magnitude ($3 \, \mu_{\rm B}$), while the whole structure exhibits zero net magnetization and lacks long-range magnetic order.
\end{comment}
%
First, we modeled the paramagnetic (PM) state by using the $3 \times 3 \times 1$ supercell (18 Cr atoms) and constructing the special quasi-random structure (SQS) \cite{Zunger1990Jul} of up and down spins to simulate magnetic disorder. More technical details on the modeling of the PM state are given in the Appendix. Using the SQS supercell we computed the spectral function, which we subsequently unfolded to the unit cell to enable direct comparison with ARPES data. The unfolding procedure is performed using the post-processing package {\sc bands4vasp} \cite{Dirnberger2021Jun} which defines the spectral function as $A(\mathbf{k},E) = \sum_m P_{\mathbf{K}m}(\mathbf{k}) \delta(E-E_m)$, where $\mathbf{k}$ $(\mathbf{K})$ is the wavevector in the BZ of the unit cell (supercell) and $P_{\mathbf{K} m}(\mathbf{k})$ projects the supercell eigenstates $\ket{{\mathbf{K}} m}$ onto the unit cell eigenstates $\ket{\mathbf{k} n}$. By calculating the spectral function rather than just the band structure, we can identify which $\mathbf{k}$-resolved states vanish due to magnetic disorder and which remain robust. This approach directly reveals how spin disorder affects different parts of the electronic structure.

To accurately reproduce the experimental band positions, we systematically evaluated various Hubbard \textit{U} and Hund \textit{J} parameters for the Cr 3\textit{d} orbitals, ultimately determining that $U=\SI{1.3}{\eV}$ and $J=\SI{0.9}{\eV}$ yielded optimal agreement of calculated spectral function with ARPES measurements. 
DFT-calculated spectral function of the PM phase matches experiments in terms of band dispersion at the VBM, high-intensity pockets between \SIrange{-1}{-2}{\eV}, and more dispersive features at higher BEs (Fig. \ref{fig:Fig1}e-f). 
Flat bands around $\Gamma$ appearing at \SI{-2}{\eV} in Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig1}e are well captured by DFT, as well as the bundle of bands at \SI{-4}{\eV} spreading throughout the BZ. We note that while the energy positions of bands can be compared between ARPES measurements and DFT-calculated spectral functions, their intensities can be only partly related. This is because of inherent DFT limitations which do not account for matrix element effects, quasiparticle excitations, electron-phonon coupling terms etc. -- all of which play a role in determining band visibility in ARPES spectra.   

To further assess how the charge distribution on metallic centers affects the VB, we acquired ARPES spectra out-of- and at-resonance with the Cr 3\textit{p} core level, displayed in Fig. \ref{fig:Fig3}a, to reveal bands with prominent Cr 3\textit{d} character. 
