At resonance the feature centered at $\sim\SI{-1.3}{\eV}$ is strongly enhanced, as highlighted also by the corresponding energy distribution curves (EDCs) in Fig. \ref{fig:Fig3}b. We attribute the increased signal to Cr states: this has been observed also in ref. \cite{DeVita2022} in a photon energy scan across the whole Cr 3\textit{p} absorption edge. We note the highly nondispersive nature of this band, albeit with some slight hints of delocalization at the boundaries of the BZ. Additionally, another weakly dispersing feature at $\sim\SI{-3.1}{\eV}$ appears enhanced at resonance. Our out-of-resonance data do not reveal this state, probably because of unfavorable matrix elements suppressing the photoemitted intensity.

To facilitate comparison between ARPES measurements when Cr states are amplified (at-resonance) and the DFT-calculated spectral function, we projected out the Cr 3\textit{d} bands (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig3}c) from the total spectral function presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig1}f. Furthermore, Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig3}d presents the atom-projected density of states (pDOS), clearly identifying the energy ranges dominated by Cr $3d$ and I $5p$ orbital contributions. By comparing the measured (\textit{E}, \textit{k}) spectra with the calculated spectral function -- Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig3}a and Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig3}c -- we notice that a high spectral weight of Cr 3\textit{d} bands is found in similar energy ranges. A strong Cr 3\textit{d} feature appears at \SI{-1.3}{\eV} in both datasets, while a weaker experimental feature appearing at \SI{-3.1}{\eV} aligns with computed Cr 3\textit{d} states centered around \SI{-2.8}{\eV}. The pDOS plot in Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig3}d reveals Cr 3\textit{d} states spanning from the VBM down to \SI{-3.6}{\eV}, with two distinct maxima (green highlights) corresponding to these features.  We stress here that the proper choice of Hubbard \textit{U} is crucial to describe the two prominent Cr 3\textit{d} features: by increasing \textit{U} the stronger feature is lowered in energy. Its positioning on the energy scale is adequately reproduced by using \SI{1.3}{\eV}; higher \textit{U} values lower the stronger feature too much, so that for $U > \SI{2.0}{\eV}$ the stronger and the weaker features merge into a single broad band (Supporting Information, Fig.~S6). We note that changing \textit{J} and taking any value for the Cr-based compounds from \SIrange{0.6}{0.9}{\eV} does not affect significantly the calculated spectral function. Spectral functions calculated for different (\textit{U}, \textit{J}) combinations are presented in the Supporting Information, Fig.~S5.

\begin{comment}
Besides telling us about the positioning of Cr $3d$ states, the ARPES measurements tell us where the I $5p$ states lay. By comparing the ARPES bands in Fig~\ref{fig:Fig2}a with calculated spectral function (Fig~\ref{fig:Fig1}f and Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig2}c) and pDOS (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig2}d) we can see that all the states located at BEs from $E_{\rm VBM}$ down to $E-E_{\rm VBM}\approx \SI{-0.8}{\eV}$ are mainly of I 5\textit{p} character, with Cr 3\textit{d} orbitals starting to give some contribution below $-0.4 {\rm eV}$. It is evident that VBM is purely of I $5p$ character, as the prominent single band of total spectral function located at VBM around $\Gamma$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig1}f) disappears on the Cr $3d$ projection (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig2}c). Apart from the states just below VBM, there are deep lying states that are also of purely I $5p$ character. These states are located between $-3.8$ and $-4.6 \, {\rm eV}$ both in measured and calculated ARPES spectra, and the fact that these states are clearly visible in ARPES measurement out of resonance but vanich in resonance is a strong indication of their I $5p$ character. 
\end{comment}

ARPES measurements not only reveal the energy positions of Cr 3\textit{d} states but also identify the I 5\textit{p} state distribution. Comparison between the ARPES bands (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig3}a) and the calculated spectral function (Figs.~\ref{fig:Fig1}f and \ref{fig:Fig3}c) and pDOS (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig3}d) shows that states from the VBM down to $E-E_{VBM} \approx \SI{-0.8}{\eV}$ are predominantly of I 5\textit{p} character, with Cr 3\textit{d} contributions starting to emerge below $\approx\SI{-0.5}{\eV}$. This is evident also from the disappearance of the prominent dispersing band at the VBM near $\Gamma$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig1}f) in the Cr 3\textit{d}-projected spectral function (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig3}c). Additionally, deeper-lying states between $\SI{-3.8}{\eV}$ and $\SI{-4.6}{\eV}$ exhibit exclusively I 5\textit{p} character. Their clear visibility in off-resonance ARPES measurements -- compared to their suppression under resonant conditions -- further confirms their I 5\textit{p} origin (Fig~\ref{fig:Fig2}a). 

We can additionally note from the pDOS plot (Fig~\ref{fig:Fig3}d) that the Cr 3\textit{d} and I 5\textit{p} states overlap in a broad range across several electronvolts, suggesting strong orbital mixing between the two, which is characteristic of covalent bonding. On the other hand, the I 5\textit{p} states between $\SI{-3.8}{\eV}$ and $\SI{-4.6}{\eV}$ do not mix with Cr 3\textit{d} states, as evident from the nearly negligible Cr 3\textit{d} DOS values in this energy range. 

