\caption{\protect\label{fig:Fig2}XAS spectra across the Cr \textit{L}\textsubscript{2,3} edges, acquired with left and right circularly polarized photons, and corresponding XMCD, at \SI{90}{\K} (\textit{top}) and at \SI{30}{\K} (\textit{bottom}). The inset shows the experimental geometry.}
\end{figure}

The overall lineshape of the XAS white line is similar to previous results on the same material \cite{Frisk2018,Kim2019}: the main \textit{L}\textsubscript{3} peak (3) is located at \SI{575.9}{\eV}, two features can be recognized both at lower (peaks 1,2) and higher (peaks 4,5) photon energies, whereas the \textit{L}\textsubscript{2} peak is characterized mainly by two features giving opposite dichroism (7,9), with a minor peak of the same dichroic character (8) and a pre-edge (6). We also observe almost no difference between $T>T_C$ and $T<T_C$ in the lineshape of the average XAS spectra (see Supporting Information Fig. S1, main panel), which suggests that sample conditions were extremely stable during the measurement, and that the difference in the XMCDs is fully attributable to intrinsic effects. To compare our data, we refer to spectra acquired previously on CrI\textsubscript{3} \cite{Frisk2018}, as well as the standard reference for pure Cr\textsuperscript{3+} valence states, the oxide Cr\textsubscript{2}O\textsubscript{3} \cite{Ito1999,Kucheyev2007,Chiou2011,Singh2012,Vazquez2018}, similar to CrI\textsubscript{3} in terms of crystal structure, nominal transition metal valence, and ligand coordination geometry. We note that the details of the lineshape and the relative intensity between peaks shows clear deviations from ref. \cite{Frisk2018}, which is in turn closer to Cr\textsubscript{2}O\textsubscript{3}. Features located in the low-energy region and in the neighborhood of pre-edges of the \textit{L}\textsubscript{3} and \textit{L}\textsubscript{2} peaks, which are crucial to correctly assess the orbital character in transition metal halides \cite{Sant2023,DeVita2024}, show the most evident differences. For example, in our case peak (1) is sharper and more pronounced, peak (2) has no equivalent, and peak (6) emerges more clearly from the pre-edge region of the \textit{L}\textsubscript{2} peak, other than presenting a non-zero dichroic signal. Lastly, the tail of the \textit{L}\textsubscript{3} peak is considerably less broad.

We also note that the \textit{L}\textsubscript{2,3} edges appear shifted towards lower photon energies compared to the typical values retrieved for Cr\textsubscript{2}O\textsubscript{3} \cite{Ito1999,Kucheyev2007,Chiou2011,Singh2012,Vazquez2018}. The observed shift suggests a higher electron count at the cation site in CrI\textsubscript{3}, albeit not strong enough to justify an outright change in valence state \cite{Theil1999}. 

From these observations we can gather that the electronic character in CrI\textsubscript{3} shows relevant deviations from a pure Cr\textsuperscript{3+}. We note that a higher number of electrons on average does not necessarily mean the presence of a mixed valence state: that would affect the lineshape of the XAS white line by considerably broadening spectral features, as seen \textit{e.g.} in mixed-valence manganites \cite{Abbate1992,DeJong2005,Werner2010,Pesquera2016}. The small signal present in the pre-edge region around \SI{573}{\eV} (Supporting Information, Fig. S1 inset), where typically Cr\textsuperscript{2+} states are located \cite{Theil1999,Ishida2008,Buccoliero2025}, is two orders of magnitude lower compared to the intensity of the main peaks, not nearly enough to justify a mixed-valence state. Instead, we interpret our data as a partial redistribution of the charge by the ligands; that is, a more \textit{covalent} character of the chemical bond due to Cr \textit{e\textsubscript{g}} -- I 5\textit{p} orbital hybridization \cite{Liu2018,Shao2021,He2025}. This is further supported by the presence of a dichroic signal at the I \textit{M}\textsubscript{4,5} edges (Supporting Information, Fig. S2), due to the mixing of Cr and I final states \cite{Kim2019}. Moreover, multiplet cluster calculations based on the Quanty code \cite{Haverkort2016} (Supporting Information, Fig. S3) show that, in absence of hybridization, the higher energy component of the main \textit{L}\textsubscript{3} peak gets stronger, and a shoulder appears on peak (3), both at odds with our data. If hybridization is taken into account in the calculations instead, a 3\textit{d} electron occupation of $n=3.52$ (Supporting Information, Table S1) is consistent with an orbital admixture of Cr and I states. We have thus solid ground to confirm the hint of hybridization suggested by the overlap between Cr 3\textit{d} and I 5\textit{p} in the pDOS in Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig3}d.

\begin{figure*}[htb]
\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{Fig4_v4}
\caption{\protect\label{fig:Fig4} (a) ARPES (\textit{E}, \textit{k}) spectra along the $\bar{\mathrm{K}}-\bar{\Gamma}-\bar{\mathrm{K}}$ direction, at $T=\SI{300}{\K}$ and $T=\SI{30}{\K}$. (b) EDCs at $\bar{\Gamma}$ as a function of temperature. The inset enhances the VBM region to highlight the change in slope. (c) EDCs at $\bar{\Gamma}$ at \SI{300}{\K} (\textit{left}) and at \SI{50}{\K} (\textit{right}). The black line is a multipeak fit as described in the main text. The colored Gaussians represent the spectral features splitting below $T_C$. (d) Calculated pDOS of the band structure in the PM (\textit{left}) and FM (\textit{right}) phases projected on Cr \textit{d} and I \textit{p} orbitals, showing the main contribution in the presented energy range. (e) Collinear DFT calculations highlighting the up- and down-spin projections of the pDOS in the near-Fermi region.}
\end{figure*}

The key question we can address now is how the charge redistribution affects the electronic bands in CrI\textsubscript{3}, and what role it plays in the magnetic transition. To better assess the modifications induced on the band structure by the PM$\rightarrow$FM transition, we acquired (\textit{E}, \textit{k}) spectra above and below the Curie temperature, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig4}a. Additional insight is also given by EDCs at the $\bar{\Gamma}$ point in Fig. \ref{fig:Fig4}b, to better illustrate the changes occurring in the band dispersion.

By comparing spectra and EDCs, we do not observe significant changes in effective masses within bands, or energy position in the non-dispersive features at high BE, indicating that the bandwidth of the VB is not strongly affected. However, the energy- and momentum-resolved information available in (\textit{E}, \textit{k}) spectra allows us to highlight more focused details. Indeed, two major modifications take place when crossing $T_C$: below the transition temperature, we can see that (i), the hole-like pocket at $\overline{\Gamma}$ at \SI{-2}{\eV}, corresponding to the most intense peak in the EDCs in Fig. \ref{fig:Fig4}b (red arrow), shifts towards higher BEs and broadens, revealing two separate components (blue arrows); and (ii), the VBM area shows a displacement of the two dispersive I 5\textit{p} bands (black arrows), translating into a noticeable broadening of the EDCs in the VBM region.

When looking at the inset in Fig. \ref{fig:Fig4}b, where the VBM region is zoomed in, the cutoff of the EDC at the VBM is sharper at higher temperatures, contrasting with the expected thermal broadening. This is emphasized by the two vertical dashed lines -- drawn in correspondence of the points of maximum change of slope in the spectrum at $T=\SI{30}{\K}$. Similarly, the broadening of the feature at \SI{-2}{\eV} at lower temperature contrasts the increased sharpness that electronic bands should exhibit at lower temperatures, and that indeed takes place in other regions of the spectrum (Supporting Information Fig.~S4).

To understand the different spectral contributions, we fitted the EDCs with Gaussian shapes superimposed to a Shirley background, focusing in particular on the region at \SI{-2}{\eV} and the VBM (Fig. \ref{fig:Fig4}c). The EDC at \SI{300}{\kelvin} shows the presence of two overlapping bands, one much broader than the other, marked by different effective masses as evident from the (\textit{E}, \textit{k}) dispersion. On the other hand, at $T=\SI{50}{\K}$ two such peaks appear on top of the broader signal. This is consistent with a splitting of $\approx\SI{450}{\milli\eV}$ of the sharper feature in two separate photoemission signals in the FM state. We notice the same effect at the VBM, where a splitting appears ($\approx\SI{250}{\milli\eV}$). It is also noteworthy that the center of mass of the I 5\textit{p} band manifold at \SI{-2}{\eV} shifts towards lower BEs by $\approx\SI{300}{\milli\eV}$. On the other hand, the shifts of other bands are comparable to our experimental bandwidth ($\approx\SI{100}{\milli\eV}$), as the shift of bands at the VBM ($\SI{60}{\milli\eV}$ shift), therefore no conclusive statement can be made. 

\begin{comment}
We depicted the valence I $5p$ bands in Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig4}b. At the $\Gamma$ point these two bands are clearly distinguishable from the rest of the band structure as they do not overlap with other bands. In the FM state, V$_1$ and V$_2$ spin-split into two bands each, as inferred from the $\sigma_z$ spin expectation values in Bloch states corresponding of these bands calculated at the $\Gamma$ point. %The exchange-induced spin splitting is sizable at $\Gamma$, $100 \, {\rm meV}$ for V$_1$ and $110 \, {\rm meV}$ for V$_2$. 
Note that the inclusion of SOC is crucial for the description of valence bands. Otherwise, without SOC, V$^{\uparrow}_1$ and V$^{\downarrow}_1$ bands, as well as V$^{\uparrow}_2$ and V$^{\downarrow}_2$), would be degenerate at $\Gamma$.
By finding the signature of FM-driven splitting onto I $5p$ orbitals, we confirm by means of an independent technique that these states hybridize with the metal centers. 
\end{comment}

The differences in EDC spectra between the PM and FM phases for the near-Fermi electronic states are well reproduced in our DFT calculations. Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig4}d shows how the single PM-phase peak splits into two distinct peaks in the FM phase (small black arrows), which we attribute to exchange-split majority and minority states of primarily I 5\textit{p} character. Collinear DFT calculations (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig4}e) confirm this interpretation: the peak nearer to the VBM consists of hybridized majority Cr 3\textit{d} and I 5\textit{p} states, while the lower-energy peak is purely minority I 5\textit{p} in character, as there are no minority Cr 3\textit{d} states close to the VBM. This strongly suggests that the ferromagnetic alignment of Cr magnetic moments, mediated by Cr \textit{e\textsubscript{g}} -- I 5\textit{p} hybridization, induces in the I 5\textit{p} states a noticeable exchange splitting, whose magnitude ($\approx\SI{200}{\milli\eV}$) is consistent with experimental findings. While significantly smaller in magnitude than the splitting of Cr 3\textit{d} states which is in electronvolt scale, this effect demonstrates the non-negligible spin polarization of ligand states through hybridized channels. For the deeper states observed in the EDC spectra -- the PM-phase single peak near \SI{-2}{\eV} that splits into two in the FM phase (Fig.~\ref{fig:Fig4}c) -- the interpretation is less straightforward. From the DFT-calculated pDOS in this energy region (\SIrange{-1.5}{-3.0}{\eV}) we can locate multiple overlapping peaks, that appear to be mixed with little distinction in the PM phase but well-separated into two main contributions in the FM phase. However, a quantification of the splitting is more challenging due to the presence of multiple contributions.

Symmetry considerations further support our claim that the long-range magnetic ordering in the FM ground state is precisely driven by the orbital mixing. The only symmetry-allowed ligand-to-metal bonds are $\sigma$-type involving \textit{e\textsubscript{g}} and $\pi$-type involving \textit{t\textsubscript{2g}}. When the spin orientation is out-of-plane (\textit{i.e.}, the preferable direction of Cr magnetic moments in bulk CrI\textsubscript{3}), $\pi$ bonding cannot occur between \textit{t\textsubscript{2g}} and spin-orbit entangled $j=1/2$ ligand states \cite{Kim2019}, leaving superexchange $\sigma$ hopping as the preferred path. We also consider that KGA rules for a pure \textit{d}\textsuperscript{3} occupation (\textit{i.e.} \textit{t\textsubscript{2g}}-\textit{t\textsubscript{2g}} interaction) with cations in octahedral sites would result in AFM coupling, whereas the exchange channel mediated by \textit{e\textsubscript{g}} orbitals (Anderson's mechanism) is primarily FM \cite{Kanamori1959,Goodenough1963,Kashin2020}. The \textit{e\textsubscript{g}} orbital occupation due to hybridization is certainly low compared to the \textit{t\textsubscript{2g}}; however, other processes beyond KGA rules can contribute to the magnetic state. In particular, as argued by Stavropoulos et al., the interaction given by \textit{e\textsubscript{g}} paths in presence of heavy ligand SOC and strong Hund's coupling is expected to drive the system into a FM state \cite{Stavropoulos2021}. The upshift of the I 5\textit{p} bands at \SI{-2}{\eV} that we experimentally observe (Fig. \ref{fig:Fig4}c-d) is likely a signature of Hund's energy gain on the ligand site, similarly to Te ligand states in CrGeTe\textsubscript{3} \cite{Watson2020}, due to the mediation of the exchange via the hybridized bonding. As a result, it is the hybridization between Cr \textit{e\textsubscript{g}} and I \textit{p} orbitals that allows the stabilization of a FM ground state in CrI\textsubscript{3}. In this regard, DFT-based calculations of the upshift \cite{Demiroglu2016} are hindered by the orbital delocalization, and thus struggle to quantitatively and reliably account for the measured energy upshift of I 5\textit{p} states. This discrepancy is expected, given the inherent limitations of DFT as a ground-state theory in describing finite-temperature electronic phenomena.

In any case, we note that our hybridized superexchange picture does not in principle exclude contributions from other mechanisms, \textit{e.g.}, \textit{t\textsubscript{2g}}--\textit{e\textsubscript{g}} hopping, which is also expected to contribute to the FM exchange \cite{Song2022,Ghosh2023}. 

Our study employs a multi-technique approach to experimentally point at a direct link between covalency, orbital hybridization and magnetic character of CrI\textsubscript{3} in terms of intralayer stabilization of ferromagnetism. The role of \textit{e\textsubscript{g}} states has been suggested by theoretical studies as the main driving force also for interlayer exchange interactions \cite{Jang2019,Song2023}, underlining the importance of a mixed orbital picture. In any case, conclusions on interlayer coupling are beyond the scope of this study. 

\section{Conclusions}

We presented a combined experimental and theoretical study of the orbital structure of CrI\textsubscript{3}, and its interplay with the emergence of long-range magnetic order in this compound. By comparing (\textit{E}, \textit{k}) non-resonant and resonant ARPES spectra with DFT calculations, we were able to benchmark the theoretical calculations in the FM and PM phases and gain insight on the band-projected orbital ordering. XAS and XMCD measurements at the Cr \textit{L}\textsubscript{2,3} and I \textit{M}\textsubscript{4,5} edges suggest a sizable orbital mixing and electron redistribution between ligand and metal, with an electron count as high as \num{3.52} electrons per Cr atom from cluster calculations accounting for hybridization. By means of (\textit{E}, \textit{k}) ARPES spectra and corresponding DFT-calculated spectral function and pDOS, we reveal multiple splittings (of the order of $\sim\SIrange{300}{400}{\milli\eV}$) opening in I-derived bands in the FM phase. Our results suggest that those electrons that are covalently redistributed by the ligand largely support the onset of the FM phase. 

The present study focuses on CrI\textsubscript{3} as a prototypical material, but the same principles generalize easily to other vdW magnets. In particular, the considerations on correlations and couplings at an orbital-specific level, framing the electronic/magnetic phase diagram, do not lose generality when extended to other 3\textit{d}-transition-metal-based systems, whenever a source of SOC in the form of a heavy ligand is provided. We expect that the role of orbital mixing and \textit{d}-orbital electronic correlations will become recognized as crucial in underpinning the magnetic properties of many of these materials.

