\paragraph{SIS Model.}
Epidemic models have seen continued interest as representations for complex interacting systems. In this context we distinguish between immunizing (such as an SIR-type model) and non-immunizing models (e.g. the presented SIS model), which concern the behavior of an agent that was infected, recovers and is then either considered immune or can get infected again. In the context of represented complexity, immunizing models are much simpler because they show a single so-called cascade of infections spreading across the network, while in a re-infecting model we can see many interactions between adjacent vertices. 

SIS models have seen wide-spread interest not only in the epidemiology domain (see \cite{nowzari_analysis_2016} for a review), but also in fields such as credit and financial market modeling \cite{chen_credit_2023, barja_assessing_2019} or rumor spreading in social networks \cite{dong_sis_2018}. Even though simplified in their interaction behavior, they capture important elements of neighborhood influence and cascading behavior, while still being theoretically analyzable. 
